,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.698846584,"data":"       | We can think of s modulo (b-1). Since s(s-1) ≡ 0 mod (b-1). This is equivalent to s ≡ 0 or 1 mod (b-1) (since (b-1) is not necessarily prime; but product of two consecutive numbers being divisible by m does not imply one factor is divisible by m unless m is squarefree? Wait property: If m is any integer, and m divides xy, it does not guarantee m divides x or y unless m is prime or relatively prime to the other factor. However, since s and s-1 are consecutive, they are coprime (gcd(s, s-1) = 1). So if m divides product of two coprime numbers, then m must divide one of them. Indeed, if gcd(s, s-1) = 1, any prime factor of m must divide either s or s-1, and since they are coprime, the whole m must divide one of them. However, m may have repeated prime factors; but since s and s-1 are coprime, they cannot share any prime factor, thus the prime powers dividing m must all be assigned to one factor. So m must divide s or s-1. Because if m = p1^{e1} p2^{e2}..., each prime power must divide either s or s-1; but could be split across both? Since s and s-1 are coprime, they cannot share any prime factor, so each prime factor appears wholly in one of them. So m can be expressed as product of two coprime numbers each dividing s or s-1. However, for m to divide the product, each prime power must be assigned to either s or s-1. Thus overall m divides s * s-1, but not necessarily s or s-1 individually; it could be that some prime powers divide s and others divide s-1, making m not dividing either individually. But because s and s-1 are coprime, the only way for m to divide product is that each prime power factor of m divides either s or s-1. So m may not divide s or s-1 wholly if its prime powers are split. Example: m=6, s=2, s-1=1 =\u003e product=2, not divisible by 6. Not good. Example: m=6, s=3, s-1=2 =\u003e product=6, divisible by 6, but 6 does not divide 3 nor 2 individually. So indeed m can divide product without dividing either factor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.698854254,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.698855598,"data":"       | Thus the condition s(s-1) ≡ 0 mod (b-1) is equivalent to (b-1) dividing product of consecutive numbers, not necessarily s or s-1 individually.\n"}
