,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.698858671,"data":"       | But we can analyze using Chinese remainder: We need s modulo each prime power dividing b-1 to be either 0 or 1 mod that prime power? Wait for each prime power p^k dividing b-1, we need p^k | s(s-1). Since s and s-1 are consecutive, they are coprime, so p^k cannot split across both; it must divide one of them fully. Therefore for each prime power p^k dividing b-1, we must have s ≡ 0 mod p^k or s ≡ 1 mod p^k. So s must satisfy a system of congruences where for each prime power factor, s is either 0 or 1 modulo that prime power. This leads to 2^{ω} possibilities where ω is number of distinct prime powers? Actually each prime power factor yields 2 choices. If b-1 has factorization ∏ p_i^{e_i}, then for each i we choose whether s ≡ 0 (mod p_i^{e_i}) or s ≡ 1 (mod p_i^{e_i}). By Chinese remainder theorem, each combination yields a unique residue modulo (b-1). So the number of residues modulo (b-1) satisfying condition is 2^{k} where k is number of distinct prime powers (i.e., number of distinct prime factors). Let's denote r = number of distinct prime divisors of b-1, i.e., ω(b-1). Then there are 2^{ω(b-1)} residues modulo (b-1) that satisfy s(s-1) ≡ 0 (mod b-1).\n"}
