,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.698866271,"data":"       | Since the interval length is at most b-1 - ceil(sqrt(b)) + 1 ≈ b - sqrt(b). That's less than b. So at most one representative per residue class may appear? Actually there could be multiple s in that interval belonging to same residue class if interval length \u003e modulus (b-1). But interval length is less than b, while modulus is b-1, so at most 2 numbers per class? Let's check: interval length ≤ b - sqrt(b) \u003c b-1 for b\u003e1? For b≥2, sqrt(b)≥1.414, so b - sqrt(b) ≤ b - 1.414 \u003c b-1 for b≥2? Actually b - sqrt(b) \u003c b-1 when sqrt(b) \u003e1, which holds for b\u003e1. So interval length \u003c b-1. Therefore the interval can contain at most one integer from each residue class modulo (b-1). So the count of s satisfying condition is simply the number of residues among those 2^{ω} that lie in the interval.\n"}
