,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.698898242,"data":"       | Observation: The maximum possible number of solutions is at most 2^{ω(b-1)} (since each subset yields at most one s). So we need 2^{ω(b-1)} \u003e 10, i.e., ω(b-1) ≥ 4 (since 2^3=8 \u003c10, 2^4=16 \u003e10). So b-1 must have at least 4 distinct prime factors. Minimal such b-1 is product of first four primes: 2*3*5*7 = 210. So b-1 ≥ 210 =\u003e b ≥ 211. However not all subsets will necessarily yield s in the interval [L, b-1]; some may fall below L. So we might need even larger b to get \u003e10 solutions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.698900652,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.698902119,"data":"       | But perhaps some b with fewer distinct primes could still have \u003e10 solutions because some subsets may produce s values both in [L,b-1] and also maybe s = b-1 counts as one solution (the subset where all residues 0). The maximum number of solutions is at most 2^{ω(b-1)} but could be less due to lower bound L. For large b, L is about sqrt(b). Since interval length is b - sqrt(b), which is close to b for large b, many residues will be in interval. For large b, roughly half of the residues (maybe) will be above sqrt(b). Actually the residues are uniformly distributed across 0..b-2. The proportion of residues \u003e= sqrt(b) is (b-1 - ceil(sqrt(b)) + 1)/(b-1) ≈ 1 - sqrt(b)/(b) = 1 - 1/√b. For large b, this proportion approaches 1. So for large b, almost all residues will be in interval. Thus the number of solutions will approach 2^{ω(b-1)} (minus maybe a few). So to get \u003e10, we need ω(b-1) at least 4, and b large enough such that at least 11 of those residues are \u003e= sqrt(b). Since 2^{4}=16, we need at least 11 residues among the 16 to be \u003e= sqrt(b). For large b, likely many will be. So minimal b may be not too large beyond 211.\n"}
