,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.698908048,"data":"       | But we also need to ensure that for each subset, the CRT solution s lies within [0, b-2] (or 0). Actually the CRT solution is a unique residue modulo b-1; we can take its least nonnegative representative in [0, b-2]. Then we can also consider s = b-1 (i.e., residue 0). So each subset yields a unique s in [0, b-1] (maybe 0 or b-1). But s = 0 is not allowed because s≥2. However the subset where all prime powers choose residue 0 leads to s ≡ 0 mod (b-1). The least nonnegative residue is 0, but we can also take s = b-1 (since adding modulus). That's allowed and gives s = b-1. So that subset yields s = b-1 which is in interval for b≥3. So we can count that.\n"}
