,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699302164,"data":"       | Given b-1 = m. Let ω(m) = number of distinct prime factors. Number of possible residues (solutions) = 2^{ω(m)}. Among them, the smallest possible s (excluding s=1) is at least 2? Actually the smallest s from residues may be 0 (converted to b-1) or 1, etc. But the lower bound L = ceil(sqrt(b)). For b up to some threshold, many residues may be below L, reducing count.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69930401,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69930513,"data":"       | We can bound the number of solutions ≤ 2^{ω(m)} - 1 (since s=1 not allowed). Actually s=0 corresponds to b-1 which is allowed. So max count is 2^{ω(m)} - (maybe 1 if s=1 is below L). So to have \u003e10, need 2^{ω(m)} \u003e= 12? Actually need at least 11 solutions. Since maximum is 2^{ω(m)} - (maybe 1). So need 2^{ω(m)} \u003e= 12, i.e., ω(m) \u003e= 4 (since 2^3=8\n"}
