,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699560604,"data":"     [Reasoning] (5581 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699561771,"data":"       | analysisThus 211 is the least.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699562935,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699564054,"data":"       | Now we should provide reasoning and perhaps analytic derivation to show why the count grows and why 211 is minimal. But we can present computational reasoning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699565497,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699566591,"data":"       | We can also derive formula for number of solutions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699567791,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699568911,"data":"       | From earlier derived condition: a = (k^2 - k)/(b-1). Since a must be integer between 1 and b-1 inclusive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699570198,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699571294,"data":"       | Thus we need (k^2 - k) divisible by (b-1). Let d = b-1. Then a = (k^2 - k)/d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699572518,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699573598,"data":"       | Since a \u003c= d, we have (k^2 - k) \u003c= d^2 =\u003e k(k-1) \u003c= d^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699574975,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699576072,"data":"       | Also a \u003e=1 =\u003e k(k-1) \u003e= d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69957733,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699578431,"data":"       | Thus d \u003c= k(k-1) \u003c= d^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699579665,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699581025,"data":"       | Also a integer =\u003e d divides k(k-1). Since k and k-1 are coprime, d must divide either k or k-1. Indeed, if d is composite with factors dividing each, but since gcd(k,k-1)=1, any prime factor of d must divide either k or k-1, not both. So d must be a divisor of either k or k-1. However, d could be product of primes each dividing either k or k-1. Since they are coprime, the whole d must divide product of k and (k-1) but cannot split across both? Actually if d = p*q where p divides k and q divides k-1, then since p and q are coprime (distinct primes), product divides k(k-1). That's possible. So d can be any integer whose prime factorization can be partitioned into two sets, one dividing k, the other dividing k-1. Equivalent to d dividing k(k-1). Since k and k-1 are coprime, this is equivalent to d = d1*d2 where d1|k, d2|k-1, and gcd(d1,d2)=1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699584961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699586102,"data":"       | But we also have bound d \u003c= k(k-1) \u003c= d^2. So k roughly sqrt(d) to d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699587444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699588545,"data":"       | Now we also need c = k - a = k - (k^2 - k)/d = (k d - (k^2 - k))/d = (k d - k^2 + k)/d = (k(d - k + 1))/d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699589808,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699590944,"data":"       | But we can compute c = k - a. Since a = (k(k-1))/d. So c = k - k(k-1)/d = k(1 - (k-1)/d) = k ( (d - (k-1))/d ) = k (d - k + 1)/d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699592278,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699593394,"data":"       | Thus c integer as well (since a integer, c integer). Since 0 \u003c= c \u003c= d, we need 0 \u003c= d - k + 1 \u003c= d/k? Wait we need c \u003c= d (i.e., b-1). Let's compute inequality: 0 \u003c= c = k(d - k + 1)/d \u003c= d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699595054,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699596154,"data":"       | Since k\u003e0, we have d - k + 1 \u003e=0 =\u003e k \u003c= d+1. Also c \u003c= d =\u003e k(d - k + 1) \u003c= d^2 =\u003e (d - k + 1) \u003c= d^2/k. Since k approx sqrt(d), this holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69959789,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699599022,"data":"       | But also we have a \u003c= d =\u003e (k(k-1))/d \u003c= d =\u003e k(k-1) \u003c= d^2 (already). So constraints reduce to: d \u003c= k(k-1) \u003c= d^2 and k \u003c= d+1 (from c \u003e=0). The latter is automatically satisfied if k(k-1) \u003e= d because for large d, k is at most d+1? Let's check.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699600887,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699601978,"data":"       | Since k(k-1) \u003e= d, we have k^2 - k - d \u003e=0 =\u003e k \u003e= (1+ sqrt(1+4d))/2 approx sqrt(d). So k is roughly between sqrt(d) and d (since k \u003c= d+1). So feasible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699603531,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699604628,"data":"       | Thus each divisor d may have multiple k satisfying divisibility condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699605861,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699607241,"data":"       | But we can perhaps count solutions for given d (i.e., b-1). The condition is that d divides k(k-1). Since k and k-1 are consecutive, the set of k modulo d that satisfy k(k-1) ≡ 0 (mod d) is exactly k ≡ 0 or 1 (mod each prime power dividing d). More precisely, for each prime power p^e dividing d, we need k(k-1) ≡ 0 mod p^e. Since p^e divides product, either p^e divides k or p^e divides k-1. So k ≡ 0 mod p^e or k ≡ 1 mod p^e. For multiple prime powers, we need a simultaneous condition: for each prime power factor p_i^{e_i} of d, choose either 0 or 1 modulo that prime power, independent of others. Thus there are 2^{ω(d)} solutions modulo d, where ω(d) is number of distinct prime factors of d. However, not all of these solutions satisfy the bounds d \u003c= k(k-1) \u003c= d^2 and k \u003c= d+1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699610032,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699611138,"data":"       | Thus the number of k in range sqrt(d) ≤ k ≤ d+1 satisfying the congruence may be approximated by about 2^{ω(d)} * (range length)/d? Actually each residue class modulo d yields about (range length)/d solutions. The range length is roughly d (since k from about sqrt(d) to d). So expected count about 2^{ω(d)} * (d/d) = 2^{ω(d)}. So the number of beautiful numbers for base b = d+1 is about 2^{ω(d)} (maybe plus some small variations). Indeed for d=210 (b=211), ω(210)=4 (since 210=2*3*5*7). 2^{4}=16, but we got 15 solutions (close). For d=30 (b=31), ω=3 (2*3*5) =\u003e 2^3=8, we got 7 solutions (close). So pattern matches.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699615,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699616158,"data":"       | Thus the count \u003e10 requires 2^{ω(d)} \u003e= 11 approx =\u003e ω(d) \u003e= 4 (since 2^3=8). So we need at least 4 distinct prime factors in d = b-1. The smallest integer with at least 4 distinct prime factors is 2*3*5*7=210. So b = 211. Indeed that yields \u003e10 solutions. For any smaller b, d=b-1 \u003c210, thus ω(d) \u003c=3, giving at most 8 solutions, but due to bounds may be less, leading to at most 7 as observed. So minimal b is 211.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699618361,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699619681,"data":"       | Thus we can produce a reasoning: The condition reduces to d | k(k-1). Since k and k-1 are coprime, each prime factor of d must divide either k or k-1, giving 2^{ω(d)} possible congruence classes modulo d. The range for k is roughly between sqrt(d) and d (since k^2 = n ≤ b^2-1 = (d+1)^2-1 = d^2+2d). Actually k ≤ d+1. So there are at most d+1 - ceil(sqrt(d)) +1 ≈ d - sqrt(d) + O(1) possible k. For each residue class we get at most one solution because the interval length is less than d? Wait interval length is about d - sqrt(d). Since each residue class repeats every d, there can be at most one solution per residue class. So total solutions ≤ number of residue classes that satisfy condition, i.e., ≤ 2^{ω(d)}. So number of beautiful numbers ≤ 2^{ω(b-1)}. And we can achieve near equality for many d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699622444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699623551,"data":"       | Thus to have more than ten, need 2^{ω(b-1)} ≥ 11 =\u003e ω(b-1) ≥ 4. The smallest b-1 with 4 distinct prime factors is 210. So b=211.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699625052,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699626121,"data":"       | We also must verify that for b=211 we indeed have \u003e10 solutions; we already enumerated 15.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699627444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699628522,"data":"       | Thus answer is 211.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699629641,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699630745,"data":"       | Now we need to craft final answer with explanation and maybe include enumeration.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699632008,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69963309,"data":"       | Let's produce final answer: \\boxed{211}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.6996343,"data":"       | \n"}
