,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699646198,"data":"     [Reasoning] (6906 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699647397,"data":"       | analysisWe need to find the least integer base b \u003e=2 such that there are more than ten b-eautiful integers. Let's parse definition:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699648791,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699649888,"data":"       | A positive integer n is b-eautiful if:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699651067,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699652168,"data":"       | - When expressed in base b, n has exactly two digits (i.e., n is between b and b^2 -1 inclusive? Actually two-digit numbers in base b are from b (i.e., 1*b + 0) up to b^2 - 1 (i.e., (b-1)*(b+1)? Wait: In base b, a two-digit number has representation: n = a*b + c where a and c are digits (0 \u003c= a,c \u003c= b-1) and a != 0 (leading digit non-zero). So n in [b, b^2 - 1] inclusive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699654022,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699655177,"data":"       | - The sum of the two digits equals sqrt(n). That is, a + c = sqrt(n). Since sqrt(n) must be integer (since a + c integer). So n must be a perfect square.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69965655,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699657664,"data":"       | Thus we need to count number of n such that:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699658821,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699659947,"data":"       | - n = a*b + c where a in {1,...,b-1}, c in {0,...,b-1}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69966149,"data":"       | - n is a perfect square: n = k^2 for some integer k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699662707,"data":"       | - a + c = k (since sqrt(n) = k). So k = a + c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699663895,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699665044,"data":"       | Thus we need integer solutions (a,c,k) with:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699666211,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699667311,"data":"       | - a in [1,b-1], c in [0,b-1], k = a + c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699668491,"data":"       | - n = a*b + c = k^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69966961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699670727,"data":"       | Thus we need to count number of integer pairs (a,c) satisfying a*b + c = (a + c)^2, with constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699671991,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.6996731,"data":"       | Let's denote a,b,c positive integer (a\u003e=1). Write equation:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699674362,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699675455,"data":"       | a*b + c = (a + c)^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699676551,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699677675,"data":"       | Expand RHS: a^2 + 2ac + c^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699678812,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699679928,"data":"       | Thus:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699683505,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699684634,"data":"       | a*b + c = a^2 + 2ac + c^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699685805,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699686904,"data":"       | Rearrange:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699688011,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699689141,"data":"       | 0 = a^2 + 2ac + c^2 - a*b - c\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69969158,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69969269,"data":"       | =\u003e a^2 + 2ac + c^2 - a*b - c = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699693905,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699695018,"data":"       | We can treat as quadratic in a or c. Let's solve for b in terms of a,c:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699696258,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699697338,"data":"       | a*b = a^2 + 2ac + c^2 - c\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69969847,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.6996996,"data":"       | =\u003e b = (a^2 + 2ac + c^2 - c) / a = a + 2c + (c^2 - c)/a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699700831,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69970194,"data":"       | Since b is integer base, we need (c^2 - c) divisible by a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69970314,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699704221,"data":"       | Alternatively solve for c in terms of a,b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699705424,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69970652,"data":"       | Let's treat equation as:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699707631,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699708704,"data":"       | a*b + c = (a + c)^2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699709807,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699710895,"data":"       | =\u003e a*b + c = a^2 + 2ac + c^2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69971207,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699713158,"data":"       | =\u003e 0 = a^2 + 2ac + c^2 - a*b - c\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699714371,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699715484,"data":"       | =\u003e c^2 + (2a -1) c + (a^2 - a*b) = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699716654,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699717725,"data":"       | Consider c as variable, quadratic:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699718854,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699719924,"data":"       | c^2 + (2a -1) c + (a^2 - a*b) = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699721091,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699722225,"data":"       | For integer solutions, discriminant must be perfect square:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699723448,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699724581,"data":"       | Δ = (2a -1)^2 - 4*(a^2 - a*b) = (4a^2 -4a +1) - 4a^2 + 4a*b = -4a +1 + 4a*b = 4a(b-1) -4a +1? Wait compute: -4a +1 + 4a*b = 4a*b -4a +1 = 4a(b-1) +1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699726014,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699727095,"data":"       | Thus Δ = 4a(b-1) + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699728275,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699729355,"data":"       | Thus discriminant must be a perfect square, say d^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699730534,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699731604,"data":"       | So we need d^2 = 4a(b-1) + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699732784,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699733872,"data":"       | Since RHS is odd (1 mod 4). So d must be odd.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699735024,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699736125,"data":"       | Let d = 2t + 1 (t integer \u003e=0). Then d^2 = (2t+1)^2 = 4t^2 + 4t +1 = 4t(t+1) +1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699738564,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699739894,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699741038,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699742138,"data":"       | 4t(t+1) + 1 = 4a(b-1) + 1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699743297,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699744361,"data":"       | =\u003e t(t+1) = a(b-1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699745518,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69974666,"data":"       | Thus we have factorization: a(b-1) = t(t+1). Since a \u003e=1 integer, b \u003e=2 integer, t \u003e=0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699748014,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699749148,"data":"       | Now, given b, we need to count number of solutions (a,c) with a in [1,b-1], c in [0,b-1] such that discriminant condition holds and c computed from quadratic formula yields integer within range.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699750597,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699751695,"data":"       | But from discriminant we can compute c:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69975287,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699753951,"data":"       | c = [ -(2a -1) ± d ] / 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699755144,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699756231,"data":"       | Since d = sqrt(Δ) = sqrt(4a(b-1)+1) = odd integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699757485,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699758605,"data":"       | Thus c = ( - (2a -1) ± d ) /2 = ( -2a +1 ± d )/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699759884,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699761018,"data":"       | We need c integer (since numerator odd +/- odd yields even? Let's check: d odd, 2a-1 odd (since 2a even minus 1 odd). So -(2a -1) = -2a+1 is odd? Actually -2a+1 is odd (since 2a even, +1 odd). Sum of two odds is even, difference of two odds is even. So c integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699762625,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699763687,"data":"       | We need c in [0, b-1].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699764807,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699765917,"data":"       | Also a must be between 1 and b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699767058,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699768171,"data":"       | Now, from discriminant condition we have t(t+1) = a(b-1). Since t(t+1) is product of consecutive integers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69976946,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699770571,"data":"       | Thus for each factorization of a(b-1) into consecutive integers product, we can find t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699771875,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699772984,"data":"       | But we also need to ensure that c computed is within range.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699774188,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699784622,"data":"       | Let's express c using t. Since d = 2t+1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699786671,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699787788,"data":"       | Plug into formula:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699788912,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699790041,"data":"       | c = (-2a + 1 ± (2t+1))/2 = (-2a + 1 ± 2t + 1)/2 = (-2a + 2 ± 2t)/2 = (-a + 1 ± t).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699792858,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699793991,"data":"       | Thus two possibilities:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699795118,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699796214,"data":"       | c1 = -a + 1 + t = t - a + 1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699797364,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699798455,"data":"       | c2 = -a + 1 - t = -(a + t -1) = -a - t +1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699799624,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699800734,"data":"       | Since c must be nonnegative, we likely take the + sign (c1) because c2 would be negative for t\u003e=0 and a\u003e=1 unless maybe t small.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699802162,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699803584,"data":"       | Let's check: c2 = 1 - a - t \u003c= 0 (since a\u003e=1, t\u003e=0). It could be zero only if a=1 and t=0. That yields c2 =0. So c2 may be nonnegative only for a=1, t=0. That yields c=0 (makes sense). So we have at most one solution using minus sign for a=1,t=0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69980525,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699806314,"data":"       | Thus main solutions are c = t - a + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699807451,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69980853,"data":"       | Thus we require:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699809627,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699810731,"data":"       | c = t - a + 1 ∈ [0, b-1].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69981195,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699813024,"data":"       | Also a ∈ [1, b-1].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69981418,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699815264,"data":"       | Now we have relation t(t+1) = a(b-1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699816421,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699817551,"data":"       | Given b, we need to find integer pairs (a,t) satisfying this, with a in [1,b-1] and resulting c = t - a + 1 between 0 and b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699818881,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699819958,"data":"       | Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699821085,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699822215,"data":"       | From t(t+1) = a(b-1). Since a \u003c= b-1, we have t(t+1) \u003c= (b-1)^2? Actually a(b-1) \u003c= (b-1)*(b-1) = (b-1)^2. So t(t+1) \u003c= (b-1)^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69982368,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699824751,"data":"       | Thus t is at most about b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699825884,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69982695,"data":"       | Now, we can try to count solutions for each b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699828124,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699829221,"data":"       | Goal: find smallest b such that number of b-eautiful integers \u003e 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69983052,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699831622,"data":"       | Each solution (a,c) corresponds to n = a*b + c = (a + c)^2 = k^2 where k = a + c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69983286,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699833991,"data":"       | But note that each (a,c) yields a unique n? Let's verify: n = a*b + c. Since representation in base b is unique, each pair (a,c) yields distinct n. Also k = a + c, but could two different (a,c) give same n? Possibly if they have same n but different representation? Not possible because representation is unique (two-digit). So each solution corresponds to a distinct b-eautiful integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699837005,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699838182,"data":"       | Thus number of b-eautiful integers equals number of solutions (a,c) satisfying constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69983944,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699840564,"data":"       | Thus we need to count number of integer pairs (a,c) (or equivalently (a,t)).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699841835,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699842952,"data":"       | Let's convert to (a,t). From t(t+1) = a(b-1). Since a,b,t integers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699844162,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699845258,"data":"       | Given b, we can iterate over possible a dividing t(t+1). Since a must be divisor of t(t+1) and also a \u003c= b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699846607,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699847748,"data":"       | But we can also think of t and a as factorization of product of consecutive numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699849168,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699850294,"data":"       | Let’s denote m = b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699851524,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699852675,"data":"       | Then equation becomes t(t+1) = a*m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699853831,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699854921,"data":"       | Thus a = t(t+1) / m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69985607,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699857361,"data":"       | Since a must be integer between 1 and m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699858574,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.6998597,"data":"       | Thus for each t, we can compute a = t(t+1)/m if divisible and within range.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699860974,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699862092,"data":"       | Also need c = t - a + 1 within [0, b-1] = [0, m].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69986328,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699864378,"data":"       | Now we can attempt to compute for each b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699865564,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699866665,"data":"       | But we need minimal b with \u003e10 solutions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699867901,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699869091,"data":"       | We can brute force for b up to some bound maybe 1000 or more. Let's do Python search.assistantanalysis to=python codedef count_beautiful(b):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699870524,"data":"       |     m = b-1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699871644,"data":"       |     count = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69987277,"data":"       |     solutions = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699873904,"data":"       |     # a from 1 to b-1 inclusive\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699875078,"data":"       |     for a in range(1, b):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699876248,"data":"       |         # solve quadratic for c: c = t - a + 1 where t(t+1) = a*m\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699878655,"data":"       |         # we can find t from discriminant condition\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699879924,"data":"       |         # compute discriminant\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699881127,"data":"       |         D = 4*a*m + 1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699882278,"data":"       |         d = int(D**0.5)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699883437,"data":"       |         if d*d != D:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699884581,"data":"       |             continue\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699885764,"data":"       |         # d is odd automatically\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699886948,"data":"       |         t = (d - 1)//2  # since d = 2t+1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699888112,"data":"       |         # verify t(t+1) = a*m\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699889261,"data":"       |         if t*(t+1) != a*m:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699890451,"data":"       |             continue\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699891604,"data":"       |         c = t - a + 1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69989278,"data":"       |         if 0 \u003c= c \u003c= m:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699893974,"data":"       |             # compute n\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.69989511,"data":"       |             n = a*b + c\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699896234,"data":"       |             # verify condition\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699897424,"data":"       |             if int(n**0.5)**2 == n and int(n**0.5) == a + c:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699898648,"data":"       |                 count += 1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699899821,"data":"       |                 solutions.append((a,c,n))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699900988,"data":"       |     return count, solutions\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699902158,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699903245,"data":"       | def find_min_b(limit=5000):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699904388,"data":"       |     for b in range(2, limit+1):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699905525,"data":"       |         cnt,_ = count_beautiful(b)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699906711,"data":"       |         if cnt \u003e 10:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699907921,"data":"       |             return b, cnt\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699909118,"data":"       |     return None\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699910294,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699911384,"data":"       | find_min_b(5000)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.699912538,"data":"       | \n"}
