,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700057413,"data":"       | analysisThus indeed 15 solutions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700058598,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700059704,"data":"       | Now we need to provide reasoning and answer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700060908,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700062265,"data":"       | But we also need to provide a thorough mathematical derivation to show why b=211 is minimal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700063533,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700064658,"data":"       | Let's attempt to derive formula for count of solutions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700067168,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700068333,"data":"       | We derived condition t(t+1) = a(b-1). For each divisor a of t(t+1) such that a \u003c= b-1 and a \u003e= 1, we can define m = b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70006978,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700070901,"data":"       | Thus we can think of pairs (t,a) where a = t(t+1)/m is integer and between 1 and m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700072171,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700073314,"data":"       | Thus for a given m, we need t such that m divides t(t+1). Since t(t+1) is product of consecutive integers, its prime factors are those of t and t+1, which are coprime.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700074714,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700075844,"data":"       | Thus m must be a divisor of t(t+1). For each divisor a = t(t+1)/m, we get a solution if c = t - a + 1 within range.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700077151,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700078283,"data":"       | But c = t - a + 1 = t - (t(t+1)/m) + 1 = t + 1 - t(t+1)/m = (t+1)(1 - t/m). Wait compute:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700079554,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700080661,"data":"       | c = t - a + 1 = t - t(t+1)/m + 1 = t + 1 - t(t+1)/m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700081847,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700082924,"data":"       | Simplify: c = (t+1)(1 - t/m) = (t+1)( (m - t)/m ) = (t+1)(m - t)/m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700084118,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700085244,"data":"       | Thus c = (t+1)(m - t)/m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70008638,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700087494,"data":"       | Since c must be integer between 0 and m, we need m | (t+1)(m - t). Since m divides product of (t+1)(m - t). Since m may share factors with each term.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700088871,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700090015,"data":"       | But we already have m | t(t+1) (since a integer). So m | t(t+1). So m divides both t(t+1) and (t+1)(m - t). Actually we can use that to guarantee c integer? Let's compute c directly using formula: c = (t+1)(m - t)/m. Since m divides t(t+1), we can write t(t+1) = a m. Then (t+1)(m - t) = (t+1)m - t(t+1) = (t+1)m - a m = m (t+1 - a). So indeed c = t+1 - a, which matches earlier expression c = t - a + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700091848,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700092964,"data":"       | Thus integer condition holds automatically if a integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700094197,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700095281,"data":"       | Now we need c \u003e=0 =\u003e t+1 \u003e= a. Since a = t(t+1)/m, condition t+1 \u003e= a is equivalent to t+1 \u003e= t(t+1)/m =\u003e multiply by m: m(t+1) \u003e= t(t+1) =\u003e (t+1)(m - t) \u003e= 0 =\u003e Since t+1\u003e0, this reduces to m - t \u003e= 0 =\u003e t \u003c= m. So t \u003c= m ensures c \u003e=0. Indeed t \u003c= m = b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700097335,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700098598,"data":"       | Also c \u003c= m: c = t+1 - a \u003c= m =\u003e t+1 - a \u003c= m =\u003e rearrange: a \u003e= t+1 - m. Since a positive anyway. For t \u003c= m, t+1 - m \u003c= 1, so a \u003e= something \u003c=1, automatically satisfied. So the only extra condition is t \u003c= m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700101571,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700102724,"data":"       | Thus solutions correspond to t such that:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700103908,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700104994,"data":"       | - t(t+1) is divisible by m (i.e., m | t(t+1)).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700106181,"data":"       | - t \u003c= m (since t up to m).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700107428,"data":"       | - Then a = t(t+1)/m (which will be integer between 1 and m because t(t+1) \u003c= m(m+1) =\u003e a \u003c= (m(m+1))/m = m+1, but need a \u003c= m. Let's check: Since t \u003c= m, t(t+1) \u003c= m(m+1). So a \u003c= (m(m+1))/m = m+1. Could be m+1 if t=m. Let's examine t=m: then t(t+1) = m(m+1). a = (m(m+1))/m = m+1 \u003e m, not allowed because a must be \u003c= m. So t=m not allowed unless m divides m(m+1) giving a=m+1 \u003e m, not allowed. So t must be \u003c m. So t \u003c= m-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700109665,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700110741,"data":"       | Thus t ranges from 0 up to m-1, such that m divides t(t+1). For each such t, we get a solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700112031,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700113124,"data":"       | But also need a \u003e=1, which holds because t(t+1) \u003e0 for t\u003e=1; t=0 gives t(t+1)=0, a=0 not allowed (a must be \u003e=1). So t must be \u003e=1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700114638,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700115744,"data":"       | Thus number of solutions for base b (with m=b-1) equals number of integers t with 1 \u003c= t \u003c= m-1 such that m | t(t+1). (Note: t=0 yields a=0, not allowed; t=m yields a=m+1 \u003e m). So we just need to count t in [1, m-1] with m dividing t(t+1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700117348,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70011845,"data":"       | Thus problem reduces to: Find smallest m+1 (i.e., b) such that there are \u003e10 integers t in {1,...,m-1} with m | t(t+1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700119834,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700120934,"data":"       | Now we can analyze the condition m | t(t+1). Since t and t+1 are coprime, m must divide product of two consecutive numbers, which are coprime. Therefore each prime factor of m must divide either t or t+1, but not both (since gcd(t,t+1)=1). So essentially we need t such that each prime power dividing m divides either t or t+1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700122624,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700123935,"data":"       | Thus for each divisor m, the set of t satisfying condition corresponds to Chinese remainder solutions where t ≡ 0 mod p_i^{e_i} or t ≡ -1 mod p_i^{e_i} for each prime power dividing m. This is similar to solving t ≡ 0 or -1 mod each prime power factor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700125623,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700126778,"data":"       | Thus number of solutions modulo m is 2^{ω(m)} where ω(m) is number of distinct prime factors, because for each prime power we have two choices (0 or -1). However, there may be overlapping solutions due to Chinese remainder theorem: each combination yields a unique residue modulo m because the conditions are independent (since moduli are coprime). So there are exactly 2^{ω(m)} solutions modulo m, i.e., residues t mod m satisfying condition. However we also need t in range 1..m-1, i.e., exclude t ≡ 0 mod m (t=0) and t ≡ -1 mod m (t=m-1). Those correspond to a=0 or a=m+1 which are invalid. So the total number of valid t in that range is 2^{ω(m)} - 2 (since we exclude the two residues 0 and -1). Wait need to verify: Among the 2^{ω(m)} residues, one is t ≡ 0 (mod m) (all primes choose 0). Another is t ≡ -1 (mod m) (all primes choose -1). Those are the two extremes. All other residues correspond to a between 1 and m-1 (since t not 0 or -1 mod m). Also we need t between 1 and m-1 inclusive, but residues are modulo m, each residue appears exactly once in that interval. So the count of t in [0,m-1] satisfying condition is 2^{ω(m)}. Excluding t=0 and t=m-1 leaves 2^{ω(m)} - 2 solutions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700131577,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700132721,"data":"       | Thus number of b-eautiful integers for base b = m+1 is N(b) = 2^{ω(b-1)} - 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700134063,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700135157,"data":"       | But we need to verify this formula matches earlier computed counts. Let's test for some b.assistantanalysis to=python codedef count_beautiful_formula(b):\n"}
