,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700403251,"data":"       | analysisWe need to understand the problem: For a given base b \u003e=2 integer, a positive integer n is b-eautiful if:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700404618,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700405794,"data":"       | - n expressed in base b has exactly two digits (i.e., n is between b and b^2 - 1 inclusive? Actually two digits means leading digit non-zero, so n ∈ [b, b^2 - 1] inclusive). Indeed, any integer with two digits in base b is of form n = a*b + c where a, c are digits with 1 ≤ a ≤ b-1 (since leading digit cannot be zero) and 0 ≤ c ≤ b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700407701,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700408825,"data":"       | - The sum of the two digits equals sqrt(n). So a + c = sqrt(n). Since sqrt(n) must be integer (because sum of digits is integer), n must be a perfect square.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700410227,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700411334,"data":"       | Thus we need n = (a + c)^2, with n = a*b + c, where 1 ≤ a ≤ b-1, 0 ≤ c ≤ b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700412724,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700413851,"data":"       | We need to count number of such n for each b. Find smallest b such that there are more than ten b-eautiful integers (i.e., at least 11). Need to output that b.\n"}
