,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.7004714,"data":"       | Now we need to count number of s values satisfying these conditions. Each such s gives a unique n = s^2, and unique (a,c) representation? Possibly there could be two-digit representation uniqueness: given s and b, a is determined, c = s - a. So each s yields at most one n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70047297,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700475275,"data":"       | Thus number of b-eautiful integers equals number of s in [ceil(sqrt(b)), b-1] such that (b-1) divides s(s-1) and a = (s^2 - s)/(b-1) satisfies 1 ≤ a ≤ b-1 and c = s - a satisfies 0 ≤ c ≤ b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700476844,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700477968,"data":"       | But note that the condition a ≤ b-1 is automatically satisfied? Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700479251,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700480598,"data":"       | Since a = (s^2 - s)/(b - 1). For s ≤ b-1, numerator s(s-1) ≤ (b-1)(b-2) = (b-1)(b-2). Dividing by (b-1) yields a ≤ b-2. So a ≤ b-2 \u003c b-1. So a ≤ b-2, which is okay (strictly less than b-1). Also a ≥? Since s ≥ ceil(sqrt(b)) ≥ 2 for b≥2. Let's compute lower bound: s(s-1) ≥ (ceil(sqrt(b))) (ceil(sqrt(b))-1). For b large, this could be less than (b-1). So a may be zero for small s? Actually a must be at least 1. So we need a \u003e= 1 =\u003e s(s-1) \u003e= b-1. Since a integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700482845,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700483948,"data":"       | Thus condition a \u003e= 1 gives s(s-1) \u003e= b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700485193,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700486295,"data":"       | But also s ≤ b-1 ensures a ≤ b-2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700487524,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700488621,"data":"       | Now c = s - a. Since a = (s^2 - s)/(b - 1). Let's compute c in terms of s and b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700489863,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700490958,"data":"       | c = s - a = s - (s^2 - s)/(b - 1) = (s(b - 1) - (s^2 - s))/ (b - 1) = (s(b - 1) - s^2 + s)/ (b - 1) = (s b - s - s^2 + s)/ (b - 1) = (s b - s^2)/ (b - 1) = s (b - s) / (b - 1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700492407,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700493655,"data":"       | Thus c = s(b - s)/(b - 1). Since 0 ≤ c ≤ b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700494894,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700496048,"data":"       | Now note that c integer requires (b - 1) divides s(b - s). Since we already have (b - 1) divides s(s - 1) from a integer condition. So we need both divisibility conditions simultaneously.\n"}
