,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700509594,"data":"       | s(b - s) = s(b-1) - s(s-1). Since (b-1) divides s(b-1), we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70051079,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700513501,"data":"       | s(b - s) ≡ -s(s-1) (mod b-1). So (b-1) | s(b - s) iff (b-1) | s(s-1). So the two conditions are equivalent. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700514985,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700516094,"data":"       | Thus we only need (b-1) | s(s-1). Then a and c automatically integers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700517345,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700518458,"data":"       | Now we also need c between 0 and b-1. Since c = s(b - s)/(b-1). For s in [1, b-1], numerator positive (since b - s \u003e 0). So c \u003e 0 unless s=0 or s=b. But s cannot be 0. So c positive. Also need c ≤ b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700520018,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700521374,"data":"       | Let's check bound: c = s(b - s)/(b - 1). Since s ≤ b-1, the product s(b - s) ≤ (b-1)*1? Actually maximum of s(b - s) occurs at s = (b)/2. The maximum is about b^2/4. Dividing by (b-1) ~ b gives about b/4. So c ≤ b/4 approx, which is ≤ b-1 for b≥2. So c ≤ b-1 automatically satisfied. Also c ≥ 0 automatically. So constraints on c are automatically satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700523238,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700524328,"data":"       | Thus the only constraints are:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700525451,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700526574,"data":"       | - s integer with ceil(sqrt(b)) ≤ s ≤ b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700527811,"data":"       | - (b-1) divides s(s-1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700529017,"data":"       | - Also a \u003e= 1 i.e., s(s-1) \u003e= b-1 (but if (b-1) divides s(s-1) and s(s-1) \u003e0, then the quotient a = s(s-1)/(b-1) \u003e= 1 automatically unless s(s-1) = 0, which cannot happen for s≥1 and s≤b-1 (except s=1 gives s(s-1)=0). So we must also exclude s=1 because then a=0, not allowed. But s=1 would give n=1, which is not two-digit in any base b≥2 (since n must be \u003e= b). So s≥ceil(sqrt(b))≥2 for b≥4? Let's compute: For b=2, sqrt(2) ≈1.414, ceil=2. So s≥2. For b=3, sqrt(3)≈1.732, ceil=2. So s≥2. So s≥2 always. So a≥1 automatically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700531424,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700532545,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to counting s in [ceil(sqrt(b)), b-1] such that (b-1) divides s(s-1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700533838,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700534948,"data":"       | Now we need to find smallest b where count \u003e 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70053617,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700537261,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute for each b, the number of s in that range satisfying that divisibility.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700538584,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700539695,"data":"       | Observation: s(s-1) is product of consecutive integers, thus divisible by many numbers. (b-1) dividing s(s-1) means that b-1 must be a factor of either s or s-1, or a product of factors across them. Since s and s-1 are coprime (gcd(s, s-1) = 1), any divisor of s(s-1) can be expressed as product of divisor of s and divisor of s-1, with no common factor. Since b-1 may have prime factorization; for it to divide s(s-1), each prime power must be wholly contained in either s or s-1 (cannot split across both because they are coprime). Thus b-1 must be of the form d1*d2 where d1 divides s and d2 divides s-1, with gcd(d1, d2)=1. But since s and s-1 are coprime, any divisor of s(s-1) can be uniquely split.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700542061,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700544484,"data":"       | Thus condition is equivalent to: there exists a factorization of b-1 into two coprime parts such that one part divides s and the other divides s-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700545938,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700547063,"data":"       | But more directly, we can think: Since s and s-1 are consecutive, for each divisor d of b-1, we need either d|s or d|s-1, but not both (since they are coprime). However for the whole product to be divisible, we need that for each prime power p^k dividing b-1, the full power must divide either s or s-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700548708,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700549801,"data":"       | Thus we can think of s modulo (b-1). Since s(s-1) ≡ 0 (mod b-1). So s ≡ 0 or 1 (mod each prime power dividing b-1). But not necessarily s ≡ 0 or 1 mod b-1 overall, because the condition may be satisfied if s ≡ 0 mod d1 and s ≡ 1 mod d2 where d1*d2 = b-1 and d1,d2 coprime. Chinese remainder theorem yields a unique solution modulo b-1 for each such factorization.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700551731,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700552874,"data":"       | Thus the number of solutions s modulo b-1 is equal to the number of ways to assign each prime power factor of b-1 to either the \"0\" side (s ≡ 0 mod p^k) or the \"1\" side (s ≡ 1 mod p^k). So each prime power factor can be assigned to either side, giving 2^{ω} solutions modulo b-1, where ω is the number of distinct prime powers (i.e., number of distinct prime factors). Actually each prime power factor p^k can be assigned to either side, giving 2^{t} solutions where t = number of distinct prime powers (i.e., number of distinct primes). Because each prime power must go entirely to either s or s-1. So there are 2^{r} residue classes modulo b-1 that satisfy s(s-1) ≡ 0 (mod b-1), where r = number of distinct prime factors of b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700555558,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700556964,"data":"       | But we also need s in range [ceil(sqrt(b)), b-1]. Since s is between sqrt(b) and b-1, which is less than b-1 (except maybe equality). So s modulo b-1 is just s itself (since s \u003c b-1). So each solution s must be one of the residues less than b-1 that satisfy condition. So number of s in that range equals number of residues less than b-1 that satisfy condition and also are \u003e= ceil(sqrt(b)). So essentially count of residues (solutions) that are \u003e= sqrt(b).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700559011,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70056012,"data":"       | Thus the total number of s satisfying condition within [1, b-1] is exactly 2^{r} (including possibly s=0? Wait s=0 not allowed because s≥2. Let's check: s=0 mod b-1 means s = 0 or b-1, but s cannot be 0 (since s≥2) and s can be b-1 (since s ≤ b-1). s = b-1 is allowed if b-1 satisfies condition (since s = b-1 =\u003e s(s-1) = (b-1)(b-2). Since b-1 divides product obviously because factor (b-1) present). So s = b-1 is always a solution (since s multiple of b-1). Similarly s=1 is also a solution (since s-1 = 0). But s=1 may be less than sqrt(b) for b\u003e1, so not counted.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700562384,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700563514,"data":"       | Thus among the 2^{r} solutions modulo b-1, they are all numbers between 0 and b-2? Actually there are exactly 2^{r} distinct residues modulo b-1 satisfying s(s-1) ≡ 0 (mod b-1). These residues are the numbers that are congruent to 0 modulo product of a subset of prime power factors, and congruent to 1 modulo product of the complementary subset. So they are between 0 and b-2? Possibly include 0 and 1 as residues. Indeed 0 corresponds to taking all prime powers assigned to s side (i.e., s ≡ 0 mod b-1). 1 corresponds to all assigned to s-1 side (i.e., s ≡ 1 mod b-1). Others are intermediate.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700567071,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700568204,"data":"       | Thus we have 2^{r} residues in [0, b-2] inclusive? Actually residues range 0 to b-2 (since modulo b-1). But also there may be residue b-1? Not possible because modulo b-1 residues are 0..b-2. However s can equal b-1, which is congruent to 0 modulo b-1. So s = b-1 corresponds to residue 0. So s=b-1 is counted as solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700569901,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700571041,"data":"       | Thus the set of s in [1, b-1] satisfying condition includes s=1 (residue 1) and s=b-1 (residue 0) and possibly other residues.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700572385,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700573508,"data":"       | Thus total number of s in [1, b-1] is 2^{r} (including s=1 and s=b-1). But we only count s \u003e= ceil(sqrt(b)). So we need to count among those residues those that are at least sqrt(b). Since residues are spread across range.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700575068,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700576364,"data":"       | Thus number of b-eautiful integers = number of s in [ceil(sqrt(b)), b-1] satisfying condition = number of residues among the 2^{r} that are \u003e= sqrt(b). Since residues are distinct, we can compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70057789,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700578977,"data":"       | Now we need to find smallest b such that this count \u003e10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700580231,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700581344,"data":"       | We can brute-force search over b up to some limit.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700582548,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700583663,"data":"       | But we can also analyze further.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700584827,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700585915,"data":"       | Observation: For b-1 having many distinct prime factors, 2^{r} grows exponentially with r. So we need at least 11 solutions in the range. Since we have at most 2^{r} total solutions (including small ones). So we need 2^{r} \u003e= 11 =\u003e r \u003e= 4 because 2^3=8 \u003c11, 2^4=16 \u003e=11. So b-1 must have at least 4 distinct prime factors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700587824,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700588934,"data":"       | Thus b-1 must be divisible by at least 4 distinct primes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700590161,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700591255,"data":"       | Smallest integer with 4 distinct prime factors is product of first four primes: 2*3*5*7 = 210. So b-1 \u003e= 210 =\u003e b \u003e= 211.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700592694,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700593781,"data":"       | But we also need that at least 11 of those residues are \u003e= sqrt(b). Since residues are spread across [0, b-2]; we can approximate that about half of them are \u003e b/2? Not exactly. But we can compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700595308,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700596428,"data":"       | If b-1 = 210, then b = 211. sqrt(b) ≈ 14.525. So we need residues \u003e= 15. Among 2^{4}=16 residues, we have 2 small residues maybe less than 15. Let's compute residues for b=211. Let's brute force compute s values satisfying condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700600078,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.7006012,"data":"       | We'll write Python to compute for b from 2 upward, count solutions s in required range.assistantanalysis to=python codedef count_beautiful(b):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700602624,"data":"       |     cnt=0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700603744,"data":"       |     sols=[]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700604893,"data":"       |     for s in range(int((b**0.5)+0.999999), b): # ceil sqrt(b)\n"}
