,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700702658,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.7007038,"data":"       | Now we should provide reasoning for why b must have at least 4 distinct prime factors, and why 211 works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700705104,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700706254,"data":"       | But we need to produce final answer: the least integer b \u003e= 2 for which there are more than ten b-eautiful integers is 211.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700707611,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700708721,"data":"       | Now we need to write final solution explanation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700709918,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700711028,"data":"       | Let's develop reasoning:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70071215,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70071326,"data":"       | Given b, we need to count s such that s^2 = a*b + c with a + c = s, 1 ≤ a ≤ b-1, 0 ≤ c ≤ b-1. Derived condition s(s-1) divisible by b-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700714678,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700715784,"data":"       | Proof:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700716918,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700718048,"data":"       | - Write n = s^2, representation digits a (tens) and c (units) in base b: n = a*b + c, with a + c = s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700719371,"data":"       | - Substituting c = s - a: s^2 = a*b + s - a = a(b-1) + s → s(s-1) = a(b-1). So (b-1) | s(s-1). Conversely, if (b-1) divides s(s-1) and define a = s(s-1)/(b-1), c = s - a, then a,b,c satisfy constraints, giving a b-eautiful integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700720951,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700722053,"data":"       | Thus s must satisfy divisibility.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700723193,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700724351,"data":"       | Since gcd(s, s-1) = 1, each prime power dividing b-1 must divide either s or s-1 wholly. Let b-1 = ∏ p_i^{e_i}. For each i, choose whether p_i^{e_i} divides s or s-1. This gives 2^{r} possibilities where r = number of distinct primes dividing b-1. Hence there are exactly 2^{r} solutions for s modulo b-1, i.e., in the set {0,1,...,b-2}. The two trivial solutions are s ≡ 0 (i.e., s = b-1) and s ≡ 1 (i.e., s = 1). All other solutions lie between 2 and b-2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70072639,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700727525,"data":"       | Now the condition that n has exactly two digits in base b means s^2 must be at least b, i.e., s ≥ ceil(sqrt(b)). So we only keep solutions s ≥ ceil(sqrt(b)). Since s ≤ b-1, we are looking at the subset of the 2^{r} residues that are at least sqrt(b).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700730677,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70073179,"data":"       | Thus the number of b-eautiful integers equals the number of those residues in that interval. Since among the 2^{r} residues, at most one (s=1) can be below sqrt(b) for b \u003e 4? Actually there may be more small residues depending on distribution, but we can bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700733411,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700734531,"data":"       | But to have more than ten such integers we need 2^{r} \u003e 10, i.e., r ≥ 4. Hence b-1 must have at least four distinct prime factors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700735951,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700737051,"data":"       | The smallest integer with four distinct prime factors is 2·3·5·7 = 210, giving b = 211. For this b, we can explicitly list the 2^{4}=16 residues solving s(s-1) ≡ 0 (mod 210). They are numbers congruent to 0 or 1 modulo each prime power, i.e., the set:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700738828,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700739958,"data":"       | S = {0,1, 2·3·5·7? Actually compute via Chinese remainder theorem. The residues are: 0,1, 2·3·5? Not needed. We can compute directly: they are numbers s such that for each prime p ∈ {2,3,5,7} either p divides s or p divides s-1. This yields 16 numbers between 0 and 209. They are:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700741714,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700742821,"data":"       | 0, 1, 14, 15, 20, 21, 34, 35, 70, 71, 84, 85, 104, 105, 140, 141? Wait we need to compute. Actually earlier we got solutions s in [15,210] for b=211: 15,21,36,70,85,91,105,106,120,126,141,175,190,196,210. That's 15 numbers, plus s=1 would be excluded. So there are 16 total residues including s=1. So the residues are: 1, 15,21,36,70,85,91,105,106,120,126,141,175,190,196,210 (210 ≡ 0). Indeed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700744688,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700745945,"data":"       | Now sqrt(211)≈14.5, so the residues ≥ 15 are 15,21,...,210 (15 numbers). So there are 15 b-eautiful integers for b=211, exceeding ten.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70074734,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700748434,"data":"       | We must verify that any smaller b cannot have \u003e10. Since r must be at least 4, b-1 must be at least 2·3·5·7 =210. Hence b≥211. Thus 211 is minimal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700749911,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700751011,"data":"       | But we also need to ensure that there is no b with r=4 but b-1 \u003e210 but b smaller than 211? That's impossible because b-1=210 gives b=211; any b with r≥4 must have b-1 \u003e= 210, thus b \u003e= 211. So 211 is the minimal possible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700752684,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700753761,"data":"       | Thus answer is 211.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700755078,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700756177,"data":"       | Now we can produce final answer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700757345,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700758461,"data":"       | But we also need to ensure that for b=210+1=211, there are indeed more than ten b-eautiful integers, as we have shown.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700759757,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700760857,"data":"       | Edge Cases: Could b-1 have 4 distinct prime factors but be less than 210? Not possible because product of four smallest distinct primes is 210. So minimal b-1 is 210. So minimal b is 211.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700763653,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700764754,"data":"       | Thus answer: 211.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700765865,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700766981,"data":"       | Now produce final answer with reasoning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700768148,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70076926,"data":"       | Let's craft final solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700770441,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700771531,"data":"       | assistantfinalLet  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700772668,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700773735,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700774868,"data":"       | n=a\\,b+c\\qquad(1\\le a\\le b-1,\\;0\\le c\\le b-1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700785281,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700786503,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700787674,"data":"       | be the two‑digit representation of a positive integer \\(n\\) in base \\(b\\).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700789154,"data":"       | If \\(n\\) is *\\(b\\)-eautiful* we also have  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700790443,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700791511,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700792658,"data":"       | a+c=\\sqrt n .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700793828,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700794917,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700796028,"data":"       | --------------------------------------------------------------------\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700797318,"data":"       | ### 1.  Reformulating the condition  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70079853,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700799581,"data":"       | Put  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700800703,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700801768,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700802894,"data":"       | s=a+c=\\sqrt n\\;(\u003e0).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70080411,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700805218,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700806325,"data":"       | Then \\(n=s^{2}\\) and from (1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700807581,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700808698,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700809821,"data":"       | s^{2}=a\\,b+c=a\\,b+s-a=a(b-1)+s .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700811065,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700812205,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700813274,"data":"       | Hence  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700814417,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700815491,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700816648,"data":"       | s(s-1)=a\\,(b-1) .\\tag{1}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700817923,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700819004,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70082012,"data":"       | Conversely, if an integer \\(s\\) satisfies (1) with  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700821418,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700824028,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700825178,"data":"       | a=\\frac{s(s-1)}{b-1},\\qquad c=s-a,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700826458,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700827611,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70082878,"data":"       | then \\(a\\) and \\(c\\) are digits ( \\(1\\le a\\le b-1,\\;0\\le c\\le b-1\\) ) and\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700830301,"data":"       | \\(n=s^{2}\\) is a two‑digit number in base \\(b\\).  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700831703,"data":"       | Thus\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700832804,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700833904,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700835203,"data":"       | \\boxed{\\;n\\text{ is }b\\text{-eautiful}\\iff (b-1)\\mid s(s-1)\\;}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700836578,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70083769,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700838808,"data":"       | with \\(s=\\sqrt n\\).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700840048,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700841175,"data":"       | --------------------------------------------------------------------\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700842411,"data":"       | ### 2.  When does \\((b-1)\\mid s(s-1)\\)?\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700843705,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700844817,"data":"       | Because \\(\\gcd(s,s-1)=1\\), every prime power dividing \\(b-1\\) must divide\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700846578,"data":"       | either \\(s\\) **or** \\(s-1\\) completely.  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700847875,"data":"       | Write  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700848978,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70085005,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700851201,"data":"       | b-1=\\prod_{i=1}^{r}p_i^{e_i}\\qquad(p_i\\text{ distinct primes}).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700852571,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700853675,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.7008548,"data":"       | For each prime power \\(p_i^{e_i}\\) we may choose whether it divides\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700856148,"data":"       | \\(s\\) or \\(s-1\\); the choices are independent.  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700857507,"data":"       | Hence the congruence  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700858635,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700859691,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700860823,"data":"       | s(s-1)\\equiv0\\pmod{b-1}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700861991,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700863095,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700864188,"data":"       | has exactly  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700865301,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700866424,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700867531,"data":"       | 2^{\\,r}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700868697,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700869808,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70087092,"data":"       | solutions modulo \\(b-1\\).  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700872151,"data":"       | The two trivial solutions are \\(s\\equiv0\\) (i.e. \\(s=b-1\\)) and\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70087505,"data":"       | \\(s\\equiv1\\).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700876307,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70087749,"data":"       | --------------------------------------------------------------------\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700878788,"data":"       | ### 3.  Restriction coming from the two‑digit condition  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700880038,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700881125,"data":"       | A two‑digit number in base \\(b\\) satisfies  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700882455,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700883527,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700884631,"data":"       | b\\le n=s^{2}\\le b^{2}-1\\Longrightarrow\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700885864,"data":"       | \\sqrt b\\le s\\le b-1 .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700887101,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700888214,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700889344,"data":"       | Thus we only keep those solutions of the congruence that lie in  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700890574,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700891634,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700892747,"data":"       | \\bigl[\\lceil\\sqrt b\\rceil,\\;b-1\\bigr].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700894125,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700895228,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700896393,"data":"       | Consequently the number of \\(b\\)-eautiful integers equals the number of\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700897684,"data":"       | the \\(2^{r}\\) residues that are at least \\(\\sqrt b\\).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700899034,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700900143,"data":"       | --------------------------------------------------------------------\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700901395,"data":"       | ### 4.  How many distinct prime factors are needed?\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700902601,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700903723,"data":"       | If \\(r\\le 3\\) then \\(2^{r}\\le 8\\); even before discarding the small\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700905134,"data":"       | solutions we can have at most eight candidates, so we can never obtain\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700906398,"data":"       | more than ten \\(b\\)-eautiful integers.  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70090769,"data":"       | Therefore we must have  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700908821,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700909898,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700911008,"data":"       | 2^{r}\u003e10\\Longrightarrow r\\ge4 .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70091229,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70091338,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700914478,"data":"       | Thus \\(b-1\\) must contain at least four distinct prime factors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70091581,"data":"       | The smallest integer with four distinct prime factors is  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700917001,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700918078,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700919211,"data":"       | 2\\cdot3\\cdot5\\cdot7=210 .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.70092176,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700922894,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700923997,"data":"       | Hence the smallest possible base is  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700925157,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700926301,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4070.700928024,"data":"       | b=210+1=211 .\n"}
