,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801304469,"data":"     [Turn 5]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801305499,"data":"     [Reasoning] (6169 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801306569,"data":"       | analysisThus indeed for each a from 0 to 200 inclusive, we have two permutations (a,100,200-a) and (a,200-a,100) maybe also (100,a,200-a) etc. Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801307874,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801309255,"data":"       | But the total count is 601. Let's compute expected number: For each a from 0 to 200 inclusive, we have three numbers: a, 100, 200-a. If a ≠ 100 and a ≠ 200-a (i.e., a ≠ 100 and a ≠ 100), but note a=100 gives all three equal 100. For a ≠ 100, the triple has two distinct numbers (a and 200-a) maybe equal if a=100? Actually 200-a = a when a=100. So only at a=100 we have all equal. For a ≠ 100, we have three numbers where two are distinct (a and 200-a) and also 100 distinct unless a=100 or 200-a=100 (i.e., a=100). So for a ≠ 100, the three numbers are all distinct? Let's check a=0: numbers 0,100,200 distinct. a=50: numbers 50,100,150 distinct. a=150: numbers 150,100,50 distinct (same set as a=50). So for each unordered set {a,100,200-a}, there are 6 permutations (if all three distinct). For a=100, the set {100,100,100} has only 1 permutation (all same). For a such that a=200-a (i.e., a=100) that's same case.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801311968,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801313009,"data":"       | Thus count = for a from 0 to 200 inclusive, each unordered set corresponds to a value of a (but note a and 200-a produce same unordered set). Indeed unordered set depends on unordered pair {a,200-a}. So there are 101 unordered sets? Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801314458,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801315488,"data":"       | If we consider a from 0 to 200, unordered set {a,200-a} is same as for a' = 200-a. So each unordered set appears twice except when a=100 (self). So number of distinct unordered sets = (200+1)/2 = 101 (since 0..200 inclusive, 201 numbers, pairs of two). Indeed 101 unordered sets.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801316964,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801317994,"data":"       | For each unordered set, we have permutations: If a ≠ 100, then three numbers distinct, so 6 permutations. For a=100, only 1 permutation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801319263,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801320313,"data":"       | Thus total ordered triples = 100*6 + 1 = 601. Indeed matches enumeration.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801321443,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801322437,"data":"       | Thus answer = 601.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801323477,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801324491,"data":"       | Now we need to provide reasoning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801325577,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801326603,"data":"       | We derived identity linking expression to symmetric sums: E = S1*S2 - 3*S3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801329076,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801330138,"data":"       | Given S1=300, we get 300*S2 - 3*S3 = 6,000,000 =\u003e 100*S2 - S3 = 2,000,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801331303,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801332319,"data":"       | Now we need to find integer nonnegative solutions (a,b,c) with sum 300 satisfying that.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801333695,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801334728,"data":"       | We can attempt to solve for S3 = 100*S2 - 2,000,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801335842,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801336853,"data":"       | Since S3 = abc, nonnegative integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801337916,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801339019,"data":"       | We can bound S2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801340073,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801341339,"data":"       | We have S2 = ab+bc+ca.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801342402,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801343442,"data":"       | Given a+b+c=300, we have known inequality: S2 ≤ (S1^2)/3 = 300^2/3 = 30000? Wait compute: For nonnegative numbers with fixed sum, the maximum of sum of pairwise products occurs when numbers are as equal as possible? Actually for given sum, the sum of squares is minimized when numbers equal; sum of pairwise products is (S1^2 - (a^2+b^2+c^2))/2. So maximizing S2 corresponds to minimizing sum of squares, which occurs when numbers equal (100,100,100). Then S2 = 100*100+100*100+100*100 = 3*10000 = 30000. So S2 ≤ 30000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801345383,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801346395,"data":"       | Also S2 ≥ 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801347441,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801348501,"data":"       | Thus S3 = 100*S2 - 2,000,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801349571,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801350596,"data":"       | If S2 = 30000, then S3 = 100*30000 - 2,000,000 = 3,000,000 - 2,000,000 = 1,000,000 = abc for (100,100,100). Indeed 100*100*100 = 1,000,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801351884,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801352908,"data":"       | If S2 smaller, S3 may become negative; but abc must be nonnegative, so we need S2 ≥ 20,000 (since 100*S2 - 2,000,000 ≥ 0 =\u003e S2 ≥ 20,000). So S2 must be between 20,000 and 30,000 inclusive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801354356,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801355369,"data":"       | Now we need to find integer triples with sum 300 and pairwise sum product S2 in that range, also satisfying abc = 100*S2 - 2,000,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801356629,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801357676,"data":"       | We can attempt to solve.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801358723,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801359732,"data":"       | Observation: Suppose one variable is 100. Let a=100, then b+c=200. Compute S2 = 100b + 100c + bc = 100(b+c) + bc = 100*200 + bc = 20,000 + bc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801361001,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801362038,"data":"       | Then abc = 100* b * c = 100 bc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801363096,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801364147,"data":"       | Plug into relation: abc = 100*S2 - 2,000,000 =\u003e 100 bc = 100*(20,000 + bc) - 2,000,000 = 2,000,000 + 100 bc - 2,000,000 = 100 bc. Identity holds for any b,c with sum 200. So any triple with one coordinate 100 automatically satisfies the equation! Indeed because plugging a=100 yields identity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801367824,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801368851,"data":"       | Similarly any triple where any variable equals 100 works. That explains the pattern.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80137001,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801371082,"data":"       | Now we must ensure that any solution must have at least one variable equal to 100. Let's prove.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801372272,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801373561,"data":"       | Suppose none of a,b,c equals 100. Then we can attempt to derive contradiction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801374738,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801375781,"data":"       | Let’s assume a,b,c are nonnegative integers, sum 300, none equal 100.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801376936,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801377987,"data":"       | Define variables shifted: let x = a-100, y = b-100, z = c-100. Then x+y+z = 0. So x+y+z = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80137915,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801380164,"data":"       | Then we can express condition? Let's compute expression in terms of x,y,z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801381299,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801382304,"data":"       | But maybe easier: Use derived relation: abc = 100*S2 - 2,000,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801383442,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801384446,"data":"       | If none equals 100, we can attempt to bound abc relative to S2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801385584,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801386626,"data":"       | We have S2 = ab+bc+ca.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801387673,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801388693,"data":"       | Let’s use substitution: Let a = 100 + x, b = 100 + y, c = 100 + z, with x+y+z = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801389853,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801390878,"data":"       | Then compute abc = (100 + x)(100 + y)(100 + z) = 1,000,000 + 100^2(x+y+z) + 100(xy+yz+zx) + xyz = 1,000,000 + 100(xy+yz+zx) + xyz because sum zero eliminates linear term.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801392181,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801393214,"data":"       | Similarly S2 = (100 + x)(100 + y) + (100 + y)(100 + z) + (100 + z)(100 + x) = 3*100^2 + 100(x+y+z)*2? Let's compute: Expand: (100 + x)(100 + y) = 10,000 + 100(x+y) + xy. Summing cyclically:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801394569,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801395624,"data":"       | S2 = 3*10,000 + 100[(x+y)+(y+z)+(z+x)] + (xy+yz+zx) = 30,000 + 100[2(x+y+z)] + (xy+yz+zx) = 30,000 + 200*0 + (xy+yz+zx) = 30,000 + (xy+yz+zx).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801396903,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801397915,"data":"       | Thus S2 = 30,000 + (xy+yz+zx).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801398988,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801400024,"data":"       | Now compute RHS: 100*S2 - 2,000,000 = 100*(30,000 + (xy+yz+zx)) - 2,000,000 = 3,000,000 + 100(xy+yz+zx) - 2,000,000 = 1,000,000 + 100(xy+yz+zx).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801402471,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801403517,"data":"       | Thus condition abc = 100*S2 - 2,000,000 becomes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801404604,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80140564,"data":"       | 1,000,000 + 100(xy+yz+zx) + xyz = 1,000,000 + 100(xy+yz+zx) =\u003e xyz = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801406851,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801407888,"data":"       | Thus xyz = 0, meaning at least one of x,y,z is zero, i.e., at least one of a,b,c equals 100.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801409055,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801410069,"data":"       | Thus any solution must have a variable equal 100. Conversely any triple with a variable 100 works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801411257,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801412264,"data":"       | Thus the solution set is exactly all triples with a+b+c=300 and at least one variable equals 100.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801413646,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801414958,"data":"       | Now we need to count number of ordered triples (a,b,c) of nonnegative integers with sum 300 and at least one coordinate equals 100.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801416251,"data":"       | \n"}
