,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801665814,"data":"       | Similarly Σ a_i^3 = (a+b+c)^3 - 3 (a+b+c)(ab+bc+ca) + 3 abc = s1^3 - 3 s1 s2 + 3 s3 = 27,000,000 - 900 s2 + 3 s3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801667026,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801668038,"data":"       | Plug into condition: 300 p - q = 6,000,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801669105,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801670129,"data":"       | Compute 300 p = 300 (90000 - 2 s2) = 27,000,000 - 600 s2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801671249,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801672289,"data":"       | Then 300 p - q = (27,000,000 - 600 s2) - (27,000,000 - 900 s2 + 3 s3) = (-600 s2 + 900 s2) - 3 s3 = 300 s2 - 3 s3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801673523,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801674577,"data":"       | Thus condition simplifies to 300 s2 - 3 s3 = 6,000,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801675668,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801676673,"data":"       | Divide by 3: 100 s2 - s3 = 2,000,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801677741,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801678799,"data":"       | Thus we have s2 = (2,000,000 + s3)/100 = 20,000 + s3/100.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801679932,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801680966,"data":"       | Since s2 is integer, s3 must be multiple of 100. Let s3 = 100 k, where k is integer \u003e=0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801682188,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801683158,"data":"       | Then s2 = 20,000 + k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801684174,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801685211,"data":"       | Now we have a,b,c nonnegative integers with sum 300, sum of pairwise products s2 = 20,000 + k, and product s3 = 100 k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801686427,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801687441,"data":"       | We need to find all triples (a,b,c) satisfying these.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801688569,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801689598,"data":"       | Now we also have constraints from Vieta: For given s1,s2,s3, a,b,c are roots of cubic x^3 - s1 x^2 + s2 x - s3 = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801690818,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801691821,"data":"       | But we need integer nonnegative solutions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801692894,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801693906,"data":"       | Thus we need to find integer nonnegative triples with sum 300 and product divisible by 100, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801696356,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801697387,"data":"       | But we have relation s2 = 20,000 + k, s3 = 100 k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801698546,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801699607,"data":"       | Thus k = s3/100.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801700633,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801701637,"data":"       | Now we have s2 = 20,000 + s3/100.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801702719,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801703738,"data":"       | Now we can attempt to find all nonnegative integer triples satisfying these.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801704908,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801706197,"data":"       | We can treat k as unknown integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801707268,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801708329,"data":"       | We also have inequality constraints: For nonnegative a,b,c with sum fixed, the maximum product occurs when numbers are equal: about (100)^3 = 1,000,000. So s3 \u003c= 1,000,000. Since s3 = 100 k, k \u003c= 10,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801709813,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801710901,"data":"       | Also s2 maximum? For given sum, maximum sum of pairwise products occurs when numbers are equal: s2_max = 3 * (100*100) = 30,000. Indeed s2 = ab+bc+ca \u003c= 30,000. Our formula s2 = 20,000 + k, so we need 20,000 + k \u003c= 30,000 =\u003e k \u003c= 10,000. That's consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801712598,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801713633,"data":"       | Also s2 minimum? For nonnegative numbers, min s2 is 0 (when at most one nonzero). So need 20,000 + k \u003e= 0 =\u003e always true.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801715079,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80171611,"data":"       | Thus k ranges from 0 to 10,000 inclusive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801717181,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801718293,"data":"       | Now we need to count number of nonnegative integer triples (a,b,c) with sum 300 and product 100 k and pairwise sum 20,000 + k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801719541,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801720566,"data":"       | But perhaps we can find a more direct param.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801721669,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801722696,"data":"       | Observation: The expression simplifies to 100(ab+bc+ca) - abc = 2,000,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80172385,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801724851,"data":"       | Because we derived: 100 s2 - s3 = 2,000,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801725943,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801726994,"data":"       | Thus condition: 100(ab+bc+ca) - abc = 2,000,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801728096,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801729094,"data":"       | Now we have a+b+c=300.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801730135,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801731141,"data":"       | We can attempt to solve for possible values.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801732265,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80173327,"data":"       | Let’s define a,b,c nonnegative integers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801734353,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801736827,"data":"       | We can try to factor something: Maybe rewrite as (100 - a)(100 - b)(100 - c) = ?\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80173801,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801739081,"data":"       | Let’s compute product (100 - a)(100 - b)(100 - c). Expand:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801740959,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801741958,"data":"       | Let x = 100 - a, y = 100 - b, z = 100 - c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801743031,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80174409,"data":"       | Then a = 100 - x, etc. Sum condition: (100 - x)+(100 - y)+(100 - z) = 300 =\u003e 300 - (x+y+z) = 300 =\u003e x+y+z = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801745358,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801746396,"data":"       | Thus x+y+z = 0. Since a,b,c are nonnegative, x,y,z can be positive, zero, or negative? Since a can be up to 300, x can be negative. But maybe we can find expression.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801747693,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80174869,"data":"       | Compute product: (100 - a)(100 - b)(100 - c) = ?\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801749776,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801750784,"data":"       | We can expand using symmetric sums.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80175233,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801754082,"data":"       | Let’s compute using sympy to see if expression equals something like 1,000,000 - ... Actually compute:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801755869,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801757514,"data":"       | (100 - a)(100 - b)(100 - c) = 100^3 - 100^2 (a+b+c) + 100 (ab+bc+ca) - abc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801759212,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801760883,"data":"       | Since (x - a)(x - b)(x - c) = x^3 - x^2 s1 + x s2 - s3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801762483,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801763933,"data":"       | Set x=100, s1 = a+b+c = 300, s2 = ab+bc+ca, s3 = abc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801765651,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801767336,"data":"       | Thus product = 1,000,000 - 10,000*300? Wait compute: 100^3 = 1,000,000. Then -100^2 * s1 = -10,000 * 300 = -3,000,000. Then +100 * s2 = 100 s2. Then - s3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80176925,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801770783,"data":"       | So (100 - a)(100 - b)(100 - c) = 1,000,000 - 3,000,000 + 100 s2 - s3 = -2,000,000 + (100 s2 - s3).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801772737,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801774379,"data":"       | But we have condition 100 s2 - s3 = 2,000,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801784911,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801786727,"data":"       | Thus product = -2,000,000 + 2,000,000 = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801788571,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801790208,"data":"       | Thus (100 - a)(100 - b)(100 - c) = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801792004,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801793751,"data":"       | Great! So the condition is equivalent to (100 - a)(100 - b)(100 - c) = 0 given a+b+c=300.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801795541,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80179716,"data":"       | Thus at least one of a,b,c equals 100.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801800551,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801801662,"data":"       | Now we need to count triples of nonnegative integers with sum 300 where at least one variable equals 100.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801802915,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801804019,"data":"       | But also need to satisfy the original expression, which we derived is equivalent to that condition. Let's verify: The derivation used only the given sum and expression, leading to product zero. Conversely, does product zero guarantee original expression? Let's check: If (100 - a)(100 - b)(100 - c) = 0 and a+b+c=300, does it imply 100 s2 - s3 = 2,000,000? Let's compute: Expand product expression we derived: product = -2,000,000 + (100 s2 - s3). So product zero =\u003e 100 s2 - s3 = 2,000,000. That's exactly our condition. So equivalence holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801805969,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801806998,"data":"       | Thus condition reduces to \"at least one of a,b,c equals 100\". Since sum is 300, if one variable is 100, the other two sum to 200.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801808298,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801809318,"data":"       | Thus we just need to count ordered triples (a,b,c) of nonnegative integers with a+b+c=300 and at least one variable equals 100.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801810549,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801811578,"data":"       | We can compute count via inclusion-exclusion.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801812673,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80181398,"data":"       | Let total number of ordered triples with sum 300: N_total = C(300+3-1,3-1) = C(302,2) = 302*301/2 = 45451? Let's compute: 302*301 = 909... Actually compute precisely.assistantanalysis to=python code302*301//2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801815319,"data":"     [Code]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801816372,"data":"       | 302*301//2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801817394,"data":"     [Output]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801818407,"data":"       \u003e 45451\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801819451,"data":"       \u003e \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801820491,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801821461,"data":"     [Turn 4]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801822518,"data":"     [Reasoning] (3983 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801823579,"data":"       | analysisNow count triples where a=100. Then b+c = 200, nonnegative. Number of ordered pairs (b,c) = 201 (b from 0 to 200). So count_A = 201.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801824873,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801825908,"data":"       | Similarly for b=100: count_B = 201.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80182698,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801827992,"data":"       | Similarly c=100: count_C = 201.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801829055,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801830132,"data":"       | But we have double counted triples where two variables equal 100. Let's count those: a=100, b=100 =\u003e c = 100 (since sum 300). That's a single triple (100,100,100). Similarly permutations? Actually if a=100 and b=100, then c must be 100. That's same triple. So there is exactly 1 triple where at least two variables equal 100, which is the triple (100,100,100). This triple is counted three times in the above counts (once for each variable). So we need to subtract overcount.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801833541,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801834654,"data":"       | In inclusion-exclusion: Number with at least one equals 100 = sum singles - sum doubles + sum triples (if any). Here there is only one double intersection (a=100 and b=100) etc. Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801836032,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801837067,"data":"       | Number where a=100 and b=100 =\u003e c=100. That's one triple. Similarly a=100 and c=100 same triple, b=100 and c=100 same triple. So there are 3 double intersections, each same triple (100,100,100). So sum over double intersections = 3*1 = 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801838548,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801839572,"data":"       | Number where a=100,b=100,c=100 is triple intersection = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801840698,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801841714,"data":"       | Thus total = 3*201 - 3*1 + 1 = 603 - 3 + 1 = 601.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801842819,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801843806,"data":"       | Matches enumeration.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801844891,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801845886,"data":"       | Thus answer 601.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801846929,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801847961,"data":"       | Now we need to present reasoning: Show transformation to product condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801849084,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801850076,"data":"       | Let's craft solution:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801851096,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801852106,"data":"       | Given sum S=300, expression E = Σ_{sym} a^2 b = Σ_i a_i^2 (S - a_i) = S Σ a_i^2 - Σ a_i^3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801853388,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80185453,"data":"       | Compute using identities: Σ a_i^2 = S^2 - 2(ab+bc+ca). Σ a_i^3 = S^3 - 3 S (ab+bc+ca) + 3 abc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801855768,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801856766,"data":"       | Plug in S=300, equate to 6,000,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801857825,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801858822,"data":"       | Simplify to 100(ab+bc+ca) - abc = 2,000,000.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801860083,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801861126,"data":"       | But note that (100 - a)(100 - b)(100 - c) = 100^3 - 100^2 S + 100(ab+bc+ca) - abc = 1,000,000 - 10,000*300 + 100(ab+bc+ca) - abc = -2,000,000 + (100(ab+bc+ca) - abc).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801862546,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801863555,"data":"       | Thus condition is equivalent to (100 - a)(100 - b)(100 - c) = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80186467,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801865686,"data":"       | Thus at least one variable equals 100.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801866764,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801867786,"data":"       | Since a+b+c=300, the other two sum to 200, giving 201 possibilities for each choice of which variable is 100.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801868964,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80186999,"data":"       | Use inclusion-exclusion to avoid double counting of (100,100,100). Count = 3*201 - 3*1 + 1 = 601.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801872453,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801873513,"data":"       | Thus answer is 601.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80187457,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801875598,"data":"       | Now produce final answer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801876662,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801877655,"data":"       | assistantfinal\\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801878736,"data":"       | \\begin{aligned}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801879813,"data":"       | a+b+c \u0026=300 ,\\\\\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801880959,"data":"       | E\u0026=a^{2}b+a^{2}c+b^{2}a+b^{2}c+c^{2}a+c^{2}b =6\\,000\\,000 .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801882148,"data":"       | \\end{aligned}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801883192,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801884239,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801885225,"data":"       | ---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801886221,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801887257,"data":"       | ### 1.  Rewrite the second condition\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801888299,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80188931,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801890348,"data":"       | E=\\sum_{\\text{sym}}a^{2}b\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801891475,"data":"       |    =\\sum_{i=1}^{3}a_i^{2}\\bigl((a+b+c)-a_i\\bigr)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801892694,"data":"       |    =300\\sum a_i^{2}-\\sum a_i^{3}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801893807,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801894829,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801895808,"data":"       | Use the identities  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801896858,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801897865,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801898883,"data":"       | \\sum a_i^{2}= (a+b+c)^{2}-2(ab+bc+ca),\\qquad\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801900025,"data":"       | \\sum a_i^{3}= (a+b+c)^{3}-3(a+b+c)(ab+bc+ca)+3abc .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801901163,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.8019022,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801903191,"data":"       | With \\(a+b+c=300\\),\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801904315,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801905303,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801906333,"data":"       | \\begin{aligned}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801907408,"data":"       | 300\\sum a_i^{2}\u0026=300\\bigl(300^{2}-2(ab+bc+ca)\\bigr)=27\\,000\\,000-600(ab+bc+ca),\\\\[2mm]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801908848,"data":"       | \\sum a_i^{3}\u0026=300^{3}-900(ab+bc+ca)+3abc\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801910123,"data":"       |             =27\\,000\\,000-900(ab+bc+ca)+3abc .\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801911318,"data":"       | \\end{aligned}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801912369,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801913374,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801914374,"data":"       | Hence  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801915428,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.801916425,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4109.80191877,"data":"       | E= \\bigl(27\\,000\\,000-600s_{2}\\bigr)-\\bigl(27\\,000\\,000-900s_{2}+3s_{3}\\bigr)\n"}
