,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331325738,"data":"       | analysisWe need number of subsets of {1,...,10} that contain exactly one pair of consecutive integers. That is among the elements of the subset, there is exactly one unordered pair {i,i+1} where both appear, and no other consecutive pair. The subset can have any size, at least 2 because need a pair. Could be larger.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331327345,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331328396,"data":"       | We need to count subsets S ⊆ [10] such that the number of i with {i,i+1} ⊆ S is exactly 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331329713,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331330863,"data":"       | We can approach combinatorially.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331332008,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331333024,"data":"       | Let’s define consecutive pairs positions i=1..9. For each i, indicator whether both i and i+1 are in S. We need exactly one i where both are present.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331334405,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331335584,"data":"       | But there may be overlapping pairs: e.g., if S contains {i,i+1,i+2}, then there are two consecutive pairs: (i,i+1) and (i+1,i+2). So that would be disallowed because we need exactly one pair. So we cannot have three consecutive numbers because that would create two pairs. Also cannot have two disjoint pairs (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) with j≥i+2 (non-overlapping) because that would be two pairs. Also cannot have overlapping pairs (i,i+1,i+2) as said.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331337467,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.33133851,"data":"       | Thus the subset must contain exactly one adjacent pair, and all other elements must be isolated (no adjacency with any other element). So the subset consists of a distinguished adjacent pair, plus any number (maybe zero) of other elements that are not adjacent to each other nor to the pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331340051,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331341488,"data":"       | Thus we can treat the pair as a \"block\" of size 2 occupying positions i,i+1. The remaining positions (the other numbers) cannot be selected if they are adjacent to any selected element. Since we have a block of two consecutive numbers, the numbers i-1 and i+2 cannot be selected (if they exist) because they'd be adjacent to the pair (i,i+1). Also any other selected numbers must be at least distance 2 apart from each other and from the block.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331345014,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331346111,"data":"       | Thus we are essentially counting subsets of the remaining numbers after removing the block and its immediate neighbors (i-1,i+2) (if within 1..10) such that no two selected numbers are consecutive. That's a classic problem: number of subsets of a set of size n with no consecutive elements is F_{n+2} (Fibonacci). But we also have to consider that the set of remaining numbers is not contiguous because we removed a block and its neighbors, leaving possibly two intervals: left side (1..i-2) and right side (i+3..10). Within each interval, we need to select subsets with no consecutive elements (within that interval). Also there is no adjacency across the gap because the gap includes at least one missing element (i-1 or i+2). So selections in left and right intervals are independent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.33134849,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331349523,"data":"       | Thus for each possible position i of the pair (i from 1 to 9), we need to count number of subsets of left interval of length L = i-2 (since numbers 1..i-2 are allowed) with no consecutive numbers, times number of subsets of right interval of length R = 10-(i+2) = 8 - i (since numbers i+3..10 are allowed). Actually compute: right interval start = i+3, end =10, length = 10-(i+3)+1 = 8 - i? Let's verify: if i=1, right interval = 4..10 length 7 = 8-1 =7 correct. If i=8, right interval = 11..10 none length 0 = 8-8=0 correct. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331351445,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331352498,"data":"       | So total for each i: f(L) * f(R) where f(n) = number of subsets of {1,...,n} with no consecutive elements (including empty set). Known formula: f(n) = F_{n+2}, where F_1 = 1, F_2 = 1 (standard Fibonacci). Let's verify: n=0 -\u003e subsets of empty set =1, formula gives F_2 =1 correct. n=1 -\u003e subsets: {} , {1} =2, formula F_3 =2 correct. n=2 -\u003e subsets with no consecutive: {},{1},{2}=3, formula F_4=3 correct. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331354374,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331355412,"data":"       | Thus f(L) = Fib(L+2), f(R)=Fib(R+2). Multiply and sum over i=1..9.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.33135656,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331357708,"data":"       | Thus total = Σ_{i=1}^{9} Fib(i)??? Wait compute: L = i-2, so L+2 = i. So f(L) = Fib(i). Similarly R = 8 - i, so R+2 = 10 - i. So f(R) = Fib(10 - i). So total = Σ_{i=1}^{9} Fib(i) * Fib(10 - i).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331359192,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331360226,"data":"       | Thus total = Σ_{i=1}^{9} F_i * F_{10-i}. (where F_1=1, F_2=1, F_3=2,...). Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331361438,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331362467,"data":"       | We can compute manually or using python.\n"}
