,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331521885,"data":"       | analysisWe need to count subsets of {1,...,10} that contain exactly one pair of consecutive integers. That is, among all pairs (i,i+1) with i=1..9, exactly one such pair appears as both elements in the subset. Also we must ensure that no other consecutive pair appears. However a subset could contain longer runs like three consecutive numbers; that would contain two pairs (i,i+1) and (i+1,i+2). So those are not allowed because they have more than one pair. So the subset must contain exactly one adjacent pair, and all other elements are isolated (no adjacency to any other selected element). Also the pair itself cannot be part of a larger run; i.e., the elements adjacent to the pair (i-1 and i+2) must not be selected. So we need subsets where there is a unique adjacency.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331524211,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331525288,"data":"       | We need to count subsets of size k (any k) that satisfy condition. Equivalent to choose a pair of consecutive numbers to be the unique adjacent pair, and then choose any subset of the remaining numbers such that none of them are adjacent to each other nor to the pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.33152679,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331527785,"data":"       | Let's formalize.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331528862,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331529923,"data":"       | Let the set be {1,...,n} with n=10. We need number of subsets S ⊆ [n] such that there exists exactly one i with {i,i+1} ⊆ S, and for all j ≠ i, {j,j+1} ⊄ S. Equivalent: adjacency graph on line; we need exactly one edge present in induced subgraph.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.33153149,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331532537,"data":"       | We can treat the pair as a \"block\" of size 2 that is selected, and the rest of the elements are selected as isolated points not adjacent to each other nor to the block.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331533967,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331539085,"data":"       | We can think of \"forbidden adjacency\" constraints: can't select any two consecutive numbers except the designated pair. Also cannot select numbers adjacent to the pair (i-1 and i+2) because that would create another adjacency with the pair (i-1,i) or (i+1,i+2). So those must be excluded.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331540775,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331541877,"data":"       | Thus after fixing the pair at positions i,i+1, we must exclude i-1 and i+2 from selection (if they exist). Then we can select any subset of the remaining numbers (the rest of the line after removing those four positions? Actually we remove the pair and its immediate neighbors). But we must also ensure that among the remaining numbers, no two consecutive are selected (since that would create another adjacency). So we need to count subsets of a set of numbers that are not adjacent (i.e., independent sets) on a path graph after removing certain vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.33154384,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331544908,"data":"       | Thus approach: For each possible i (1..9) for the pair, we consider the remaining positions after deleting i,i+1,i-1,i+2 (where defined). The remaining positions form up to two intervals (left of i-1 and right of i+2). Within each interval, we can select any subset with no adjacent elements (i.e., independent sets of a path). The total number of such subsets is product of counts for each interval (since intervals are independent). Then sum over i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331546815,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331547855,"data":"       | But careful: If i=1, then i-1 doesn't exist; similarly i=9, i+2=11 doesn't exist. So intervals adjust.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331549066,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331550078,"data":"       | Let's compute.\n"}
