,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.33170171,"data":"       | Alternative approach: Represent subset with exactly one adjacency as a binary string of length 10 where exactly one occurrence of \"11\" and no other adjacent 1's. Also the \"11\" cannot be part of longer run, so the pattern must be ...0110... where the block \"11\" is surrounded by zeros (or edges). So we can think of constructing binary strings of length 10 with exactly one occurrence of \"11\" and no overlapping \"11\". Equivalent to having exactly one block of two consecutive 1's and all other 1's isolated (singletons) separated by at least one 0 from each other and from the block.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331704127,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331705214,"data":"       | Thus we can treat the block \"11\" as a special object of length 2, and each isolated 1 as object of length 1, and zeros as separators. The condition: between any two 1-objects (including block) there must be at least one zero. Also at ends, no restriction (could start with 1). So we can transform to counting arrangements of objects.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.331708545,"data":"       | \n"}
