,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332025926,"data":"       | analysisWe need to find number of subsets of {1,...,10} that contain exactly one pair of consecutive integers. Means among the elements of the subset, there is exactly one pair (i,i+1) both present, and no other consecutive pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.33202741,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332028472,"data":"       | But careful: If subset contains three consecutive numbers, say {3,4,5}, then it contains two consecutive pairs: (3,4) and (4,5). That's not allowed. So exactly one pair of consecutive integers, meaning exactly one adjacency among the chosen elements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.33202992,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332030963,"data":"       | Also note that a pair could be part of larger block? The condition \"exactly one pair of consecutive integers\" means there is exactly one unordered pair {i,i+1} such that both are in the subset. So any subset where there are three consecutive numbers would have at least two pairs, thus not allowed. So subsets must not contain any block of length \u003e=3, and must contain at most one adjacent pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332032769,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332033818,"data":"       | Thus we need to count subsets of {1,...,10} such that the adjacency graph (where vertices are numbers, edges between consecutive numbers) has exactly one edge selected (both endpoints in subset) and no other edges selected.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332035219,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332036262,"data":"       | Alternatively we can think of constructing subsets with no adjacent elements (i.e., independent set) except we allow exactly one adjacency (i,i+1). So we can treat the pair as a \"double\" block of size 2 that we include, and the rest of elements must be selected such that none are adjacent to each other or to the pair (except the pair's internal adjacency). Also we cannot have any other adjacency among the rest.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332039817,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332041057,"data":"       | Thus we can approach combinatorially: Choose which consecutive pair (i,i+1) will be the unique adjacency. There are 9 possible pairs (1-2,2-3,...,9-10). For each chosen pair, we need to select a subset of the remaining 8 elements (the other numbers) such that none of them are consecutive to each other, and also none are consecutive to the chosen pair's elements (i.e., cannot select i-1 nor i+2, because that would create another adjacency with i or i+1). Also cannot select both i-1 and i+2 simultaneously? Actually each individually would create an adjacency with the pair, which is not allowed because we would have more than one pair. So we must avoid selecting any element that is adjacent to either i or i+1. So we must exclude i-1 (if exists) and i+2 (if exists) from being selected. So effectively we have a reduced set of allowed elements: the original set minus the pair and its immediate neighbors (if any). Then from those allowed elements we must select a subset with no adjacent elements (i.e., independent set) because any adjacency among them would create another pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332043896,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332044982,"data":"       | Thus for each pair we count the number of subsets of the allowed elements with no consecutive numbers. The allowed elements are the numbers from 1..10 except {i,i+1,i-1,i+2} (where defined). The remaining elements are separated into blocks (segments) of consecutive numbers (gaps) where we can select subsets with no adjacency. The count for each block of length L is the number of independent subsets of a path of length L (i.e., number of subsets of {1..L} with no consecutive numbers). That's known to be F_{L+2} (Fibonacci). Because the number of binary strings of length L with no consecutive 1's is F_{L+2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332047042,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332048085,"data":"       | Thus we can compute for each i (1..9) the lengths of the remaining blocks after removing i,i+1,i-1,i+2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332049336,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332050392,"data":"       | Let's do case analysis.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332051533,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332052582,"data":"       | Let’s define the set S = {1,...,10}. Choose pair (i,i+1). Remove also i-1 (if i\u003e1) and i+2 (if i+1\u003c10). So we have up to 4 removed numbers (some may be out of range). The remaining numbers form up to two intervals: left side of i-1 and right side of i+2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332054178,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332055207,"data":"       | Specifically:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332056255,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332057321,"data":"       | - Left side: numbers from 1 up to (i-2) (since i-1 removed). So left block length L_left = max(0, i-2). Because if i-2 \u003e= 1, then block length = i-2; else 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332058692,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332059845,"data":"       | - Right side: numbers from (i+3) up to 10 (since i+2 removed). So right block length L_right = max(0, 10 - (i+2)) = max(0, 8 - i). Wait compute: i+3 to 10 inclusive length = 10 - (i+3) +1 = 8 - i. So L_right = max(0, 8 - i). For i up to 8, positive; for i=9, L_right = -1? Actually i=9 =\u003e i+3 =12 \u003e10, so no right block, length 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332061553,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332064328,"data":"       | Thus for each i, we have two independent blocks of sizes L_left and L_right. The number of subsets with no adjacency in each block is F_{L+2}. Since the two blocks are independent, total subsets = product of counts for left and right blocks.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332065995,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332067058,"data":"       | But also note that we could also have the empty subset (choose no additional elements) which is counted as product of F_{L_left+2} * F_{L_right+2} includes the case where we select none from both blocks (since each block's count includes empty set). So that's fine.\n"}
