,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332324141,"data":"       | analysisWe need number of subsets of {1,...,10} that contain exactly one pair of consecutive integers. That means among the elements of the subset, there is exactly one unordered pair {i,i+1} such that both i and i+1 are in the subset, and no other consecutive pair appears. Also note that a subset may have more than two elements; could have longer runs? But if there is a run of length \u003e2, then it contains multiple consecutive pairs (e.g., {3,4,5} has pairs (3,4) and (4,5) two pairs). So such subsets are not allowed. So the subset can contain at most a run of length 2, and that run must be the unique consecutive pair. All other elements must be isolated (no adjacency to any other element). Also cannot have another pair of consecutive numbers elsewhere.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332326498,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332327852,"data":"       | Thus we need to count subsets of {1,...,10} where there is exactly one adjacent pair, and all other elements are isolated (no adjacency). Also we must ensure that isolated elements are not adjacent to the pair's elements (except the pair itself). For example, if the pair is (i,i+1), we cannot also have i-1 or i+2 in the subset because that would create another consecutive pair (i-1,i) or (i+1,i+2). So the subset must avoid those. So we can think of selecting a \"block\" of size 2 (consecutive pair) placed somewhere, and then selecting any subset of the remaining numbers that are not adjacent to each other nor adjacent to the block.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332330085,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332331149,"data":"       | Essentially we have a linear arrangement of 10 positions. Choose a block of two consecutive positions to be \"selected\" as the unique consecutive pair. Then we must select any subset of the remaining positions such that no two selected positions are adjacent, and also none are adjacent to the block (i.e., positions i-1 and i+2 cannot be selected). So we need to count number of subsets of the remaining positions with the restriction that they are a set of isolated positions (no adjacency) and also not adjacent to the block.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332333158,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332334223,"data":"       | Alternatively we can treat the block as a \"selected element\" of size 2, and we need to count subsets of the other numbers with no adjacency, and also cannot pick numbers adjacent to the block.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.33233568,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332336902,"data":"       | We can approach combinatorially: For each possible location of the block (i,i+1) where i ranges from 1 to 9. For each such block, we need to count number of subsets of the remaining numbers (excluding i,i+1) that have no consecutive numbers and also exclude i-1 and i+2 (if they exist). So effectively we have a line of length 10 with a forbidden block of length 2 (occupied) and also the two neighboring positions become forbidden (cannot be selected). So we have a reduced set of \"available positions\" where we can select any subset with no adjacency among themselves (since they are isolated from each other and from the block). However note that the available positions are separated into possibly two segments: left side of the block (positions 1 to i-2) and right side (positions i+3 to 10). Within each segment, we can select any subset with no two consecutive numbers. Since the two segments are independent, the total number for a given block is product of counts for each segment.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332341136,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332342215,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to: For a segment of length L (consecutive numbers) where we can select any subset with no two consecutive numbers (i.e., independent sets in a path graph of length L). The number of such subsets is F_{L+2}, where F_n is Fibonacci numbers with F_1=1, F_2=1? Let's recall: Number of independent sets of a path of length L (L vertices) is F_{L+2} (where F_0=0, F_1=1). For L=0 (empty segment) count =1 (empty set). Indeed F_2=1? Wait compute: For L=0, we have 1 independent set (empty). F_{0+2}=F_2. If we define Fibonacci with F_0=0, F_1=1, then F_2=1. Good. For L=1, independent sets: {} or {v} =\u003e 2. F_{1+2}=F_3=2. Good. So formula holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332344468,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332345647,"data":"       | Thus for each block at position i (i,i+1), we have left segment length L_left = i-2 (since positions 1..i-2 are available). Right segment length L_right = 10 - (i+2) = 8 - i? Wait compute: positions i+3..10 inclusive count = 10 - (i+2) = 8 - i? Let's compute: i from 1 to 9. Example i=1: block (1,2). Left segment length i-2 = -1? Actually there is no left segment; length zero. So we need to treat max(0,i-2). Similarly right segment length = 10 - (i+2) = 8 - i. For i=1, right length = 7. Let's verify: positions 4..10? Wait i=1 block (1,2). Forbidden positions: 0 (nonexistent) and 3 (i+2). So available right side positions start at 4? Actually i+3 = 4. So positions 4..10 inclusive count = 7. Yes 8 - i = 7. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332347883,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332348911,"data":"       | For i=9 block (9,10). Left length = i-2 = 7. Right length = 8 - i = -1 =\u003e zero. So treat zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332350197,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332351497,"data":"       | Thus for each i, number of subsets = F_{(i-2)+2} * F_{(8-i)+2} = F_{i} * F_{10 - i}. Wait compute: left length L_left = i-2 (if i\u003e=2 else 0). Then number of independent sets = F_{L_left+2}=F_{i}. Since i-2+2 = i. Good. For i=1, left length negative; but we treat left segment length 0 =\u003e count 1 = F_{2}=1? Actually for i=1, left length =0 (since no positions before i-2). So we need to treat left count =1 = F_{2}=1. But using formula F_i with i=1 gives F_1=1. That's also correct. Wait F_1=1. So left count = F_i works for i=1? Let's verify: i=1 =\u003e left length = i-2 = -1? Not allowed. But we treat left side as empty set, count =1. F_i = F_1 = 1. So works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332353793,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332354846,"data":"       | Similarly right length = 8 - i (if i \u003c=7 else 0). Number of independent sets = F_{right length+2} = F_{(8-i)+2} = F_{10 - i}. For i=9, right length = -1 =\u003e treat as 0 =\u003e count 1 = F_{?}. F_{10-9}=F_1=1. Works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332356363,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332357671,"data":"       | Thus total for block i is product: F_i * F_{10 - i}.\n"}
