,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332542012,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332543127,"data":"       | Thus we need to count subsets where we select a single \"adjacent pair\" (k,k+1) with k from 1 to 9, ensure k-1 and k+2 are not selected, and then we can select any subset of the remaining numbers that does not contain any consecutive pair. Also we must ensure that the selected subset does not create a new pair with the pair's elements; but we already forbid k-1 and k+2, so any other elements are at distance at least 2 away from the pair. However they could be consecutive among themselves; we must avoid that. So we need to count subsets of the remaining numbers (i.e., the set {1,...,10} minus {k-1,k,k+1,k+2}) that have no consecutive pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332545358,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332546543,"data":"       | But note that the removal of k-1 and k+2 may split the remaining set into up to three intervals (left side before k-1, middle gap between k+2 and maybe something else? Actually after removing k-1 and k,k+1,k+2, we have left side: {1,...,k-2} (if k≥3). Right side: {k+3,...,10} (if k+3 ≤10). So there are two intervals, not three: left interval up to k-2, right interval from k+3 to 10. The numbers k-1 and k+2 are excluded. So we need to count subsets of these intervals with no consecutive numbers within each interval (and also across intervals they are far apart, so no cross adjacency). So the total number for a given k is product of number of subsets of left interval with no consecutive numbers times number for right interval.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332550476,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332551542,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to counting number of subsets of {1,...,n} with no consecutive integers (i.e., independent sets of a path graph). This is known to be F_{n+2} (Fibonacci numbers) where F_1=1,F_2=1. Let's verify: For n=0 (empty set), there is 1 subset (empty). For n=1, subsets with no consecutive: {},{1} =\u003e 2 = F_3 = 2. For n=2: subsets: {},{1},{2} =\u003e 3 = F_4 = 3. For n=3: subsets: {},{1},{2},{3},{1,3} =\u003e 5 = F_5 =5. So indeed count = F_{n+2} where F_1=1,F_2=1.\n"}
