,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332794516,"data":"       | analysisWe need to find the number of subsets of {1,...,10} that contain exactly one pair of consecutive integers. That means among all elements of the subset, there is exactly one pair (i,i+1) that are both in the subset, and no other consecutive pairs. Also note that a subset could contain more than two elements; we just need exactly one adjacent pair. For example {1,2,5} qualifies: pair (1,2) is present, no other consecutive numbers. {1,3,6,7,10} qualifies: pair (6,7) is present, no other consecutive numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332796413,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332799276,"data":"       | We need to count subsets of the 10-element set with exactly one adjacent pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332800458,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332801524,"data":"       | Interpretation: A subset S ⊆ {1,...,10} such that there exists exactly one integer k where both k and k+1 are in S, and for all other i, not both i and i+1 are in S. Also note that if the subset contains three consecutive numbers, say {3,4,5}, then it contains two consecutive pairs (3,4) and (4,5) — that would be counted as two pairs, not allowed. So we cannot have three consecutive numbers. Also we cannot have two disjoint pairs like {1,2,5,6}. That would be two pairs, not allowed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332803403,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332804437,"data":"       | Thus we need subsets with exactly one adjacency.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332805545,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332806581,"data":"       | We can approach combinatorially.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332807712,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332808779,"data":"       | Let’s think of representing subsets as binary strings of length 10 (positions 1..10). A \"1\" indicates the element is present. The condition: there is exactly one occurrence of the pattern \"11\" (adjacent ones), and there are no overlapping occurrences (i.e., we cannot have \"111\" because that would contain two overlapping \"11\" patterns). Also we cannot have two separate \"11\"s. So the binary string must contain exactly one occurrence of \"11\", and all other 1s must be isolated (i.e., not adjacent to any other 1). Also there cannot be any other adjacency.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332811122,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332812172,"data":"       | Thus the string consists of isolated 1s (with zeros on both sides) and exactly one block of length 2 of consecutive 1s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.33281342,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332814657,"data":"       | Also we must ensure that isolated 1s are not adjacent to the block of length 2. That means there must be zeros separating them.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332815922,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332816964,"data":"       | Thus we can think of constructing strings of length 10 over alphabet {0,1} with exactly one run of length 2 of 1s, and all other runs of 1s are of length 1, and runs are separated by at least one 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332818324,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332819353,"data":"       | We need to count number of such strings.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332820443,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332821495,"data":"       | Let’s denote the block of length 2 as a \"double\" (D). The other isolated 1s as \"singletons\" (S). The string is comprised of zeros and these blocks, with the condition that any two blocks (including D and S) must be separated by at least one zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332823178,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332824246,"data":"       | Thus we can treat each block as an object occupying some positions: D occupies 2 consecutive positions, S occupies 1 position. Between any two blocks there is at least one zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332825565,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332826617,"data":"       | We need to count all subsets (i.e., strings) with any number of singletons (including zero) besides the D block, such that total length ≤10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332829611,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332830689,"data":"       | Let’s approach by counting placements of D and S's.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332831827,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332832861,"data":"       | Let k be the number of isolated 1s (singletons) in the subset besides the double pair. So total number of 1s = k + 2 (the double). k can be 0 up to? The maximum number of isolated 1s such that we can fit them with D and required zeros.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332834308,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.33283536,"data":"       | We need to place k S's and 1 D into length 10 with at least one zero between any two blocks.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332836555,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332837621,"data":"       | This is a classic stars and bars with gaps.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332838724,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332839826,"data":"       | We can treat each block as an item of length: D length 2, each S length 1. So total occupied length = 2 + k*1 = k+2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.33284106,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332842101,"data":"       | We need to insert zeros between and possibly at ends.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332843264,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332844389,"data":"       | Let’s denote gaps: there are (number of blocks + 1) gaps of zeros: before first block, between blocks (k+1 gaps between D and each S), after last block. Since we require at least one zero between any two blocks, the internal gaps must have at least 1 zero. The end gaps (before first and after last) can have zero or more zeros.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.33284625,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332847307,"data":"       | Number of blocks = k + 1 (since D plus k S). So internal gaps = (k+1 - 1) = k gaps that are between blocks (since there are k+1 blocks, there are k internal gaps). Wait: Actually with (k+1) blocks, there are (k) internal gaps (between consecutive blocks). Yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332848791,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332849807,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.33285083,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332851852,"data":"       | - k internal gaps each must have at least 1 zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332853026,"data":"       | - Two external gaps (leftmost and rightmost) each can have ≥0 zeros.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332854297,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332855327,"data":"       | Let total zeros = 10 - (k+2) = 8 - k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332856443,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332857502,"data":"       | We need to distribute these zeros into (k+2) gaps? Actually total gaps = (k+1) blocks =\u003e gaps = (k+2) gaps? Let's compute: For n blocks, there are n+1 gaps (including ends). With (k+1) blocks, there are (k+2) gaps: left end, k internal between each adjacent pair, right end. Yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332859241,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.3328603,"data":"       | So there are (k+2) gaps: left end (gap0), internal gaps (gap1,...,gapk), right end (gap_{k+1}).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332861528,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332862578,"data":"       | Constraints: internal gaps (k of them) must have at least 1 zero; external gaps can be zero or more.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332865646,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332866703,"data":"       | Let’s denote zeros in each gap: x0, x1,...,xk, x_{k+1}. Then constraints:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332867908,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332868957,"data":"       | - For i=1..k: xi ≥ 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332870088,"data":"       | - x0 ≥ 0, x_{k+1} ≥ 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332871238,"data":"       | - Sum_{i=0}^{k+1} xi = total zeros = 8 - k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332872374,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332873423,"data":"       | We need to count number of integer solutions to this.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332874637,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332875667,"data":"       | Let’s set yi = xi for i=0, k+1; and for internal gaps define zi = xi - 1 ≥ 0. Then sum becomes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332876947,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332877973,"data":"       | x0 + x_{k+1} + Σ_{i=1}^{k} (zi + 1) = 8 - k\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332879167,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332880205,"data":"       | =\u003e x0 + x_{k+1} + Σ_{i=1}^{k} zi + k = 8 - k\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332881428,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332882448,"data":"       | =\u003e x0 + x_{k+1} + Σ_{i=1}^{k} zi = 8 - 2k\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332883675,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332884672,"data":"       | Thus we need nonnegative integer solutions to:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.3328858,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332886823,"data":"       | x0 + x_{k+1} + Σ_{i=1}^{k} zi = 8 - 2k\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332887992,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332889026,"data":"       | Number of variables: (k+2) variables (x0, x_{k+1}, and k zi). All nonnegative.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332890195,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332891227,"data":"       | Number of solutions = C((8 - 2k) + (k+2) - 1, (k+2) - 1) = C(8 - 2k + k+1, k+1) = C(9 - k, k+1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332892445,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332893637,"data":"       | Because formula for number of nonnegative integer solutions to sum of m variables = N is C(N+m-1, m-1). Here m = k+2 variables, N = 8 - 2k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332894946,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332895949,"data":"       | Thus count = C(N + m - 1, m - 1) = C((8 - 2k) + (k+2) - 1, (k+2) - 1) = C(8 - 2k + k + 1, k+1) = C(9 - k, k+1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332897187,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332898297,"data":"       | But we also need to consider ordering of blocks: we have D (double) and k S's (singletons). The arrangement of these blocks along the line matters: we need to count distinct strings, which correspond to permutations of the blocks. Since D is distinguishable from S's, and S's are identical (each single 1). So we need to count number of ways to order the blocks: we have (k+1) blocks total, with 1 D and k S's. The number of distinct orderings = (k+1)! / (k! * 1!) = k+1. Because we can choose the position of D among the (k+1) blocks.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332900477,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332903057,"data":"       | Thus for each k, total number of strings = (k+1) * C(9 - k, k+1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332904263,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332905324,"data":"       | Now we must sum over possible k such that the binomial makes sense (nonnegative). Also we need 8 - 2k \u003e= 0 because zeros count must be nonnegative. So 8 - 2k \u003e= 0 =\u003e k \u003c= 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332906879,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332907912,"data":"       | Also k \u003e= 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4137.332909053,"data":"       | \n"}
