,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603060166,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse problem: We have geometric sequence terms a * r^{n-1} where a, r are positive integers, r\u003e1. Bill picks out k different numbers in this sequence, all of which have the same number of digits. What is the largest possible value of k? So we need maximum size of a subset of terms of geometric progression (with integer base a and integer ratio r\u003e1) such that all selected terms have same number of digits (in base 10). Since a and r can be chosen arbitrarily (positive integers), we need to maximize over all possible a, r (with r\u003e1) the maximum number of terms of the sequence that can have same digit length. Equivalent to: For given a,r, consider set of n such that floor(log10(a r^{n-1}))+1 = D (some digit count). For each D, there may be multiple n values. We want max count of n for any D. Over all a,r, maximize that count.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603064056,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603066253,"data":"       | We need largest possible k (finite). Since r\u003e1, the terms grow exponentially, so digit length increases eventually. But there may be intervals where digit count stays same for multiple consecutive n. The question: For integer a,r, how many consecutive terms can stay within same power-of-10 interval? That is, we need max length of interval of n where a r^{n-1} lies between 10^{d-1} and 10^{d}-1 inclusive (i.e., same digit count). This is same as max number of n such that:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603068543,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603069585,"data":"       | 10^{d-1} ≤ a r^{n-1} ≤ 10^{d} - 1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603071455,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603072595,"data":"       | Take logs: d-1 ≤ log10 a + (n-1) log10 r \u003c d\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603073873,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603074892,"data":"       | So we need number of integer n satisfying:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603076025,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603077159,"data":"       | d-1 - log10 a ≤ (n-1) log10 r \u003c d - log10 a\n"}
