,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603081813,"data":"       | Define interval I = [d-1 - A, d - A). Length = 1. So we need number of integer multiples of L (shifted by -1?) Wait (n-1)L must lie in interval of length 1. So we need to maximize number of integer k = n-1 such that k L lies in an interval of length 1. Equivalent to: For given L (positive real, log10 of integer r), what is maximal number of integer multiples of L that can fall into any interval of length 1? Since we can also shift interval by varying d (any integer). So we need to find maximal number of integer multiples of L within any length-1 interval. That's essentially the maximum number of points of arithmetic progression with step L that can be covered by a unit interval.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603086108,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603087226,"data":"       | If L is rational, say L = p/q in lowest terms (since log10 r is generally irrational unless r is a power of 10). Wait log10 r = log10 (integer r). Typically log10 r is irrational unless r is power of 10 (i.e., 10^m). But r\u003e1 integer, could be 10, 100, etc. For r=10^m, L=m integer. Then multiples are spaced integer m apart, so at most floor(1/m)+1? Actually interval length 1 can contain at most 2 multiples if spacing less than 1? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603089116,"data":"       | \n"}
