,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603315522,"data":"       | Thus we need to find largest possible length of a subsequence of consecutive terms? Not necessarily consecutive? The terms are a·r^{n-1} for n=1,2,... So we can choose any subset of terms (not necessarily consecutive). However if they all have same digit length, they must lie between 10^{d-1} and 10^{d} - 1 inclusive. Since geometric progression is monotonic increasing (a\u003e0, r\u003e1), the terms increase. So any set of terms within same digit length will be consecutive in n (since if we skip a term, the next term is larger, still within same bound). Actually could skip some terms but still within same bound; but if you skip a term, the skipped term also lies in same interval because the sequence is monotonic and the interval is contiguous; if you have term i and term j\u003ei both within interval, then all terms between i and j are also within interval because they are between them (since geometric progression increasing). So the set of terms with same digit length will be a consecutive block of terms. So k equals the maximum number of consecutive terms that can fit within a power-of-10 interval.\n"}
