mallest integer in interval. So we can achieve any length k as long as we can find m such that the interval after scaling still contains at least one integer. For m=1, scaling factor 1, interval length is 9 * 10^{d-1} which is huge, so there are many integers. So we can set a accordingly. However we also need that the terms a r^{n-1} for n up to k stay within interval. For m=1, we need a r^{k-1} \u003c= 10^{d} - 1. So we can choose a minimal possible (10^{d-1}) maybe, then condition becomes (10^{d-1}) * r^{k-1} \u003c= 10^{d} - 1. This is roughly r^{k-1} \u003c= 10 - epsilon. So condition (k-1) log10 r \u003c= log10(10 - epsilon) ≈ 1 (but slightly less). So same bound.\n"}
