,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603617516,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse problem: We have geometric series terms a * r^{n-1}, with a, r positive integers, r\u003e1. Bill picks out k different numbers in this sequence, all of which have the same number of digits. What is the largest possible value of k? So we need to find maximum number of distinct terms of a geometric progression (GP) with integer positive a, r\u003e1 integer, such that the selected terms all have same number of digits (i.e., same decimal length). We can choose any a, r, and any subset of terms (different numbers) from the infinite sequence. Want to maximize possible size of subset where all numbers have same digit count.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603619681,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.60362071,"data":"       | Essentially we want to maximize number of terms of GP that lie within a given range of decimal digits: [10^{d-1}, 10^{d} - 1] for some d. So we need to find a, r, d such that the GP hits as many numbers as possible within that interval.\n"}
