,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603623115,"data":"       | Since a and r are positive integers \u003e0, r\u003e1. The GP terms are a, ar, ar^2, ar^3,...\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603624329,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603625384,"data":"       | We need to find maximum possible number of terms in an interval of the form [10^{d-1}, 10^{d} - 1] (inclusive). Equivalent to: For some a, r, there are at most k terms such that each lies in same digit length interval. Determine maximal possible k over all a, r, d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603626858,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603628142,"data":"       | Observations: Since r is integer \u003e1, the terms increase geometrically. The ratio between successive terms is r. The length of interval for d-digit numbers is roughly factor of 10 (range width = 9*10^{d-1}). So the ratio between smallest and largest d-digit numbers is (10^{d} - 1) / (10^{d-1}) \u003c 10. So the maximum possible ratio between any two d-digit numbers is less than 10. Therefore, if we have terms a r^{i} within same digit length, then the ratio between largest and smallest among them is less than 10. Since each term is multiplied by r each step, the ratio between first and last term after m steps is r^{m-1} (if we have m terms). Actually if we have k terms, they could be not necessarily consecutive? Bill picks out k different numbers in this sequence, not necessarily consecutive. But they are all from same GP. Could be spaced apart. The ratio between smallest and largest selected terms would be r^{t} where t is difference in indices between smallest and largest. Since all terms have same digit length, ratio between smallest and largest \u003c10. So we need r^{t} \u003c 10.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.603634866,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.60363595,"data":"       | Thus the maximum difference in exponent indices (max gap) is limited by r^t \u003c10. For integer r\u003e1, we can compute maximum t such that r^t \u003c10. For r=2, t_max = floor(log_2 9) = 3 (since 2^3=8\u003c10, 2^4=16\u003e10). So we can have up to 4 terms? Wait t is difference between indices of smallest and largest. If we have k terms not necessarily consecutive, the maximum exponent gap could be up to t_max. But we could have multiple terms within that gap. The maximum number of terms we can have with exponent indices within a range of length t_max (inclusive) is t_max+1 (if we choose consecutive indices). So for r=2, max k \u003c= 4. For r=3, t_max = floor(log_3 9) = 2 (since 3^2=9\u003c10, 3^3=27\u003e10). So max k \u003c= 3. For r=4, t_max = floor(log_4 9) = 1 (4^1=4\u003c10, 4^2=16\u003e10). So max k \u003c= 2. For r\u003e=10, t_max = 0 (since r^1 \u003e=10). So only one term can be in same digit length interval. So the maximum possible k overall might be 4 for r=2, but we must also consider constraints about a such that we can have 4 terms all within same digit length. Need to see if we can achieve 4 terms with r=2.\n"}
