,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604067444,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse problem: \"The nth term of a certain geometric series is given by a·r^{n-1}, where a and r are positive integers and r \u003e 1. Bill picks out k different numbers in this sequence, all of which have the same number of digits. What is the largest possible value of k?\"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604069135,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.60407043,"data":"       | Interpretation: We have geometric progression with integer first term a and integer ratio r\u003e1. Both positive integers. Consider the set of terms of this sequence (infinite). Bill selects k distinct terms that all have same number of digits (in base 10). We need maximum possible k, over all choices of a, r, and selection of terms. Essentially, we want to find the maximum size of a subset of terms of a geometric progression that all have same digit length. Since a and r are integers, terms grow exponentially. Digit length increases eventually. But there may be intervals where multiple consecutive terms have same digit count. We need to find maximal number of terms that can have same digit count, given we can choose a and r arbitrarily (positive integers, r\u003e1). So we need to maximize the length of a block of consecutive terms (or not necessarily consecutive? \"different numbers in this sequence\" not necessarily consecutive; they could be any terms, but they all have same digit count. Since terms increase, digit count monotonic nondecreasing (strictly increasing after some point). Actually, geometric progression with integer ratio \u003e1 grows strictly, so digit count is nondecreasing and eventually strictly increasing each time it passes a power of 10. But there could be gaps: for example, terms could skip some digit lengths if ratio is large. However we can pick any subset, not necessarily consecutive. So we could pick many terms that all have same digit count, maybe not consecutive but still within same digit length interval. Since terms increase, if we pick any two terms with same digit length, they must lie within same power-of-10 interval: between 10^{d-1} and 10^{d}-1 inclusive. So the question reduces to: For a given geometric progression with integer ratio r\u003e1, what is the maximum number of terms that can lie in a given interval [10^{d-1}, 10^{d}-1] (i.e., have d digits). Over all a, r, d, we need the maximum possible number of terms that can be inside such an interval. Since we can choose a and r arbitrarily, we can try to make many terms fall into a same digit length interval.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.6040751,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604076116,"data":"       | Observation: Terms are a·r^{n-1}. Taking logs base 10: log10(a) + (n-1)·log10(r). Digit length d if floor(log10(term)) = d-1. So we need to count number of integer n such that:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604077503,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604078516,"data":"       | 10^{d-1} ≤ a·r^{n-1} ≤ 10^{d} - 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604079669,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604080676,"data":"       | Take logs: d-1 ≤ log10(a) + (n-1)·log10(r) \u003c d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604081875,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604082943,"data":"       | Thus, (n-1)·log10(r) lies within interval [d-1 - log10(a), d - log10(a)). The length of this interval is 1. So number of integer n satisfying is number of integer points of arithmetic progression (n-1)·log10(r) within a length-1 interval. Since (n-1) is integer, we consider multiples of log10(r). So we need to count how many multiples of log10(r) (including zero) lie in an interval of length 1, after shifting by -log10(a). Equivalent to: For a given real α = log10(r) \u003e 0 (since r\u003e1 integer), and shift β = -log10(a) (real), we consider the set {k·α + β : k ∈ ℕ0} and ask maximum number of points that can fall into an interval of length 1. Since we can also vary a (i.e., shift), we can align interval to maximize count.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604087494,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604088551,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to: For α = log10(r) where r is integer \u003e1, what is the maximum number of integer multiples of α that can lie in any interval of length 1? Because we can choose β arbitrarily (by choosing a). So we need the maximal possible cardinality of a subset of {0, α, 2α, 3α, ...} that can be contained in an interval of length 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.6040904,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.60409155,"data":"       | If α is rational, then multiples are periodic; if α is irrational, the fractional parts are equidistributed; but we are limited to integer multiples up to some large n? Actually infinite set; we can pick any n's. Since interval length 1, we can have at most floor(1/α)+1? Wait: The spacing between consecutive multiples is α. So in any interval of length 1, the maximum number of multiples is at most floor(1/α) + 1 (since you can fit floor(L/spacing) + 1 points). That's because you can have at most one point per spacing; the worst case is you align interval to include as many points as possible: start just before a multiple, then include that multiple and subsequent ones until exceed length 1. So maximum count = ⌊1/α⌋ + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604094001,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604095051,"data":"       | Thus we need to maximize ⌊1/α⌋ + 1 over α = log10(r) where r integer \u003e1. Since α = log10(r) \u003e0. 1/α decreases as α increases. So to maximize floor(1/α) we need α as small as possible, i.e., r as small as possible \u003e1, which is r=2. So α = log10(2) ≈ 0.3010. Then 1/α ≈ 3.3219. floor = 3. So maximum count = 3+1 = 4. So we can have at most 4 terms in same digit length interval.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604097132,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604098164,"data":"       | But we need to verify that we can indeed achieve 4 terms with some a and r=2. That would mean we can find a shift such that there are 4 multiples of log10(2) within a unit interval. Since spacing ≈0.3010, we can fit at most floor(1/0.3010)+1 = 3+1 =4 points. Need to find a such that we have 4 terms with same digit count.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.60409988,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.60410088,"data":"       | Specifically, we need to find integer a such that there exist n1 \u003c n2 \u003c n3 \u003c n4 where a·2^{n_i-1} all have same number of digits. Equivalent to find a such that there are 4 consecutive exponents (maybe not consecutive) where log10(a) + (n_i-1)*log10(2) lies in same interval of length 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604102552,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4165.604104474,"data":"       | Since we can shift by log10(a) arbitrarily (choose a). So we can align the interval to contain 4 multiples. For r=2, multiples are 0, α, 2α, 3α, ... . Need to find integer shift β = log10(a) such that there is an interval of length 1 containing 4 points: β + kα for k = m, m+1, m+2, m+3 maybe. Equivalent to there exists m such that (m+3)α - mα = 3α \u003c 1, which holds because 3α ≈ 0.903 \u003c 1. So we can place interval from β+mα - ε to β+(m+3)α + ε with ε small to include these four points. But we also need that a is integer. β = log10(a) must be log10 of integer a. So we need to find integer a such that the fractional part of log10(a) can be chosen to align. Since we can choose any integer a, its log10 may have any fractional part? Not exactly; log10(a) = log10(10^k * m) = k + log10(m) where 1 ≤ m ≤ 9 (or 1 ≤ m ≤ 9?). Actually m is integer with 1 ≤ m ≤ 9? Wait a can be any integer, not necessarily power of 10. Its log10 has integer part floor(log10(a)) = number of digits -1, and fractional part = log10(leading digits). The fractional part can be any value in [0,1) that corresponds to log10 of a number between 1 and 10 (excluding 10). Since a can be any integer, its leading digits can be any integer from 1 to 9, but also numbers like 12, 13 etc produce fractional part log10(12) - 1 = log10(1.2) ≈ 0.079. So fractional part can be any log10(m) where m∈[1,10). Since m can be any integer from 1 to 9, plus numbers like 11 produce log10(11) ≈ 1.041..., fractional part 0.041..., etc. Actually any integer a can be expressed as 10^k * t where t is integer between 1 and 9 inclusive? Not exactly; t can be any integer from 1 to 9? Wait 10^k * t where t is integer between 1 and 9 inclusive yields numbers with leading digit t and exactly k+1 digits. But numbers like 123 have leading digits 1.23? Actually 123 = 10^2 * 1.23, where t = 1.23 not integer. So representation a = 10^k * t where t ∈ [1,10). t is not necessarily integer. So fractional part of log10(a) = log10(t) where t ∈ [1,10). Since t can be any real number in [1,10) that is rational? Actually t must be integer divided by power of 10? Wait a is integer; we can write a = 10^k * m where m is integer not divisible by 10 (i.e., m ∈ {1,...,9} times some other digits). But m may have multiple digits; but after factoring out powers of 10, we get m not divisible by 10, but may be up to 9... Actually we factor out all factors of 10 (i.e., 2 and 5). For any integer a, we can write a = 10^k * b where b is integer not divisible by 10. Then b is between 1 and 9,999..., but not multiple of 10. Then t = b / 10^{digits(b)-1}? Wait we want a = 10^{k} * t where t ∈ [1,10). Since a = 10^{k} * b, where b is integer not divisible by 10, but b may be \u003e10. To get t ∈ [1,10), we can write a = 10^{k + (digits(b)-1)} * (b / 10^{digits(b)-1}) . So t = b / 10^{digits(b)-1} which is a number between 1 and 10 (not inclusive of 10). So t can be any number of the form integer divided by a power of 10, i.e., a decimal with finite digits. So fractional part of log10(a) can be any log10 of a rational number of the form b/10^{m} where b is integer not divisible by 10, and m≥0, and the result is in [0,1). This set is dense in [0,1) because numbers of that form are dense (since we can approximate any number with decimal expansions). Indeed, the fractional parts of log10(a) are dense because we can choose a with any leading digits. So we can align shift arbitrarily close to any desired fractional part.\n"}
