,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4191.578325464,"data":"       | Case 1: lcm = 12*a = 12*3x = 36x. But also lcm = 3xy. So 3xy = 36x =\u003e divide by 3x (x\u003e0): y = 12. So y=12. Since gcd(x,12)=1 (x and y coprime). So x must be coprime to 12 (i.e., not divisible by 2 or 3). Also x ≤33. Also a=3x \u003c100 =\u003e x ≤33. b=3y = 36 \u003c100 ok.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4191.57832717,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4191.57832824,"data":"       | Thus a = 3x, b=36. x must be integer ≤33, coprime to 12. Also y=12 fixed. So we need to maximize sum S = a+b = 3x + 36 = 3x+36. Since x ≤33, maximize x under coprime condition. Also need a,b\u003c100: a=3x \u003c100 =\u003e x ≤33. So largest x ≤33 with gcd(x,12)=1. Let's list numbers ≤33 coprime to 12: numbers not divisible by 2 or 3. So odd numbers not multiples of 3. Up to 33: 1,5,7,11,13,17,19,23,25,29,31. Also maybe 35 \u003e33. So max is 31? Wait 33 is divisible by 3, not allowed. 32 even, not allowed. 31 is odd not multiple of 3, okay. So x=31 gives a=93 (\u003c100). b=36. Sum=129. That's candidate.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4191.578330487,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4191.57833153,"data":"       | But also we must ensure that lcm = 12*a = 12*93 = 1116. Let's compute lcm(93,36). gcd(93,36) = 3? Let's check: 93=3*31, 36=3*12, gcd=3* gcd(31,12)=3*1=3. lcm = a*b/gcd = 93*36/3 = 93*12 = 1116 indeed matches 12*a. So works.\n"}
