,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.707957166,"data":"       | If D != 0, unique solution exists for both f(x) and f(1 - x). If D = 0, system may have either no solution or infinite solutions. Since function exists for all real numbers (presumably there is at least one solution), the system must be consistent for those x where D = 0, but not uniquely determined: infinite solutions (i.e., one degree of freedom). That corresponds to α and β? Wait: D = 0 may hold for some x values; maybe two values. Let's compute D = x^2 (1 - x)^2 - 1 = (x(1 - x))^2 - 1 = (x - x^2)^2 - 1.\n"}
