,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708129046,"data":"     [Reasoning] (5917 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708130177,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse problem: We have functional equation: for all real x,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708131453,"data":"       | x^2 f(x) + f(1 - x) = -x^4 + 2x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708132598,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708133654,"data":"       | We need to determine f(x) uniquely except at two points α and β where cannot be uniquely determined (i.e., the functional equation does not give enough info). Compute α^2 + β^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708135127,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708136396,"data":"       | Interpretation: The functional equation is a linear equation relating f(x) and f(1-x). For each x, we have one equation. For x and 1-x we get another equation. This forms a system of two linear equations in unknowns f(x) and f(1-x). Usually we can solve for both unless the coefficient matrix is singular for some x values, leading to indeterminacy. The singular points correspond to values where the system has infinitely many solutions (or no solution). Since the problem says \"uniquely determined for all values of x, except f(α) and f(β) for some real numbers α and β\", that suggests there are two values of x where the coefficient matrix is singular, leading to inability to solve for f at those points. So we need to find those x values.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708139003,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708140092,"data":"       | Let's write the system:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708141229,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708142295,"data":"       | Equation (1): x^2 f(x) + f(1 - x) = -x^4 + 2x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708143468,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708144552,"data":"       | Now substitute x -\u003e 1 - x to get equation (2):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70814579,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708146915,"data":"       | (1 - x)^2 f(1 - x) + f(x) = -(1 - x)^4 + 2(1 - x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70814808,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708149154,"data":"       | Now we have two equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708150253,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708151343,"data":"       | (1) x^2 f(x) + f(1 - x) = -x^4 + 2x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708152506,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70815362,"data":"       | (2) f(x) + (1 - x)^2 f(1 - x) = -(1 - x)^4 + 2(1 - x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708154784,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708155908,"data":"       | We can treat unknowns A = f(x), B = f(1 - x). System:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708158836,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70815995,"data":"       | x^2 A + B = -x^4 + 2x   (Eq1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708161096,"data":"       | A + (1 - x)^2 B = -(1 - x)^4 + 2(1 - x)   (Eq2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708162273,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708163416,"data":"       | Solve for A and B unless determinant zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708164592,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708165655,"data":"       | Matrix:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708166906,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708167977,"data":"       | [ x^2   1 ] [A] = RHS1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708179092,"data":"       | [ 1   (1-x)^2 ] [B] = RHS2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708180452,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708181574,"data":"       | Determinant D = x^2 * (1 - x)^2 - 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708182732,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708183962,"data":"       | If D ≠ 0, we can solve uniquely for A = f(x), B = f(1-x). If D = 0, system may be singular; may have either no solution or infinite solutions. Since the problem says \"uniquely determined for all values of x, except f(α) and f(β) for some real numbers α and β\", there must be exactly two x values where D=0, and the RHS must be consistent (i.e., the equations are dependent) so infinite solutions, leaving f(x) undetermined (maybe any value). Let's find x such that D = 0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708186074,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708187161,"data":"       | x^2 (1 - x)^2 - 1 = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708188297,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708189634,"data":"       | Let’s solve: (x (1 - x))^2 = 1 =\u003e x (1 - x) = ±1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708190976,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70819205,"data":"       | So we need to solve quadratic equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70819319,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708194298,"data":"       | x (1 - x) = 1 =\u003e x - x^2 = 1 =\u003e -x^2 + x - 1 = 0 =\u003e x^2 - x + 1 = 0 =\u003e discriminant Δ = (-1)^2 - 4*1*1 = 1 - 4 = -3 \u003c 0. No real solutions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708195819,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70819696,"data":"       | x (1 - x) = -1 =\u003e x - x^2 = -1 =\u003e -x^2 + x + 1 = 0 =\u003e x^2 - x - 1 = 0 =\u003e discriminant Δ = 1 + 4 = 5 \u003e 0. Real solutions: x = [1 ± sqrt(5)]/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708198459,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708199573,"data":"       | Thus α and β are these two real solutions: (1 + sqrt5)/2 and (1 - sqrt5)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708200852,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708203009,"data":"       | But we must verify that at these x values, the system is consistent (i.e., RHS also satisfy same relation). Because if inconsistent, there would be no solution for f at those points, which would contradict existence of function f (maybe still possible if we allow no solution? but problem says uniquely determined except f(α) and f(β) for some real numbers α and β, implying there is a function f that satisfies equation for all real x, but at those points f can be arbitrary (multiple possibilities). That would happen if the two equations are dependent (same equation) giving one constraint but not enough to determine both f(x) and f(1-x). However we have only one equation for each x; but we also have equation for 1-x which is same pair. At singular points, the two equations become linearly dependent, giving only one independent equation linking A and B. So there would be infinite possibilities for A and B satisfying that equation, but also must satisfy original functional equation for all x. Since the equation for x singular also singular for 1-x? Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708207886,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708209198,"data":"       | If D=0, then x^2 (1-x)^2 = 1 =\u003e x(1-x) = ±1. But we found only real solutions for -1 case. For these x, also 1 - x satisfies same condition? Let's compute (1 - x) (1 - (1 - x)) = (1 - x) * x = x (1 - x) = -1. So indeed same value. So for α and β, both x and 1 - x are the two solutions (they are each other's complement). Indeed α + β = 1 (since solutions to x^2 - x - 1 = 0 have sum 1). So α = (1 + sqrt5)/2 ≈ 1.618, β = (1 - sqrt5)/2 ≈ -0.618. Then 1 - α = -0.618 = β, and 1 - β = α. So they are paired.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708211482,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708212598,"data":"       | Thus for x = α, we have system:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708213764,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708214871,"data":"       | α^2 f(α) + f(β) = -α^4 + 2α (Eq1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708216158,"data":"       | f(α) + β^2 f(β) = -β^4 + 2β (Eq2) (since 1 - α = β, (1 - x)^2 = β^2, etc.)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708217518,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70821863,"data":"       | But determinant zero means these two equations are dependent (i.e., one is scalar multiple of the other). Indeed, we can verify that the coefficients matrix rows are linearly dependent: row2 = (1/α^2) * row1? Let's check: row1 = [α^2, 1]; row2 = [1, β^2]. Since α β =? Let's compute product α β =? For quadratic x^2 - x - 1 = 0, roots α, β satisfy αβ = -1 (constant term). Indeed product = -1. Also α + β = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708220675,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708221764,"data":"       | Now compute determinant: α^2 β^2 - 1 = 0 =\u003e α^2 β^2 = 1 =\u003e (α β)^2 = 1 =\u003e (-1)^2 = 1, holds. So indeed α^2 β^2 = 1. So α^2 = 1/β^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708223464,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708224573,"data":"       | Thus row1 = [α^2, 1]; row2 = [1, β^2] = [1, 1/α^2] (since β^2 = 1/α^2). So row2 = (1/α^2) * [α^2, 1] = (1/α^2) * row1. Indeed because multiply row1 by 1/α^2: (α^2)*(1/α^2)=1, 1*(1/α^2)=1/α^2 = β^2. So rows are scalar multiples, confirming dependence.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708226555,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708227626,"data":"       | Thus the two equations are same up to scalar factor. So we have only one independent equation linking f(α) and f(β). So we cannot uniquely determine each individually; there will be infinite solutions (one degree of freedom). So indeed α and β are the problematic points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708229406,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708230468,"data":"       | Thus α^2 + β^2 = ?\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70823171,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708232807,"data":"       | We can compute using known formulas: For roots of quadratic x^2 - x - 1 = 0, α + β = 1, αβ = -1. Then α^2 + β^2 = (α + β)^2 - 2αβ = 1^2 - 2(-1) = 1 + 2 = 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708234377,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70823545,"data":"       | Thus answer should be 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708238017,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708239214,"data":"       | But we must double-check that there are no other singularities: D = x^2 (1 - x)^2 - 1 = 0 leads to x(1-x) = ±1. For +1 case, no real solutions. So only two real singularities as above. So α^2 + β^2 = 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708240732,"data":"       | \n"}
