,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708619806,"data":"     [Reasoning] (3636 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70862095,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve functional equation: x^2 f(x) + f(1 - x) = -x^4 + 2x for all real x. Determine f(x) uniquely except at two points alpha, beta where cannot be determined (due to system singularities). Compute alpha^2 + beta^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708622441,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708623588,"data":"       | We have functional equation linking f(x) and f(1 - x). Let's denote equation (1): x^2 f(x) + f(1 - x) = -x^4 + 2x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708624984,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708626088,"data":"       | Also we can replace x by 1 - x: (1 - x)^2 f(1 - x) + f(x) = -(1 - x)^4 + 2(1 - x). That's equation (2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708627373,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708628458,"data":"       | We have two linear equations in unknowns f(x) and f(1 - x). Solve for f(x) (and f(1 - x)) in terms of x, unless determinant zero. Determinant: coefficient matrix [[x^2, 1], [1, (1 - x)^2]].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708629891,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708630993,"data":"       | Determinant D = x^2 * (1 - x)^2 - 1 = (x(1 - x))^2 - 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708632219,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708633288,"data":"       | If D ≠ 0, we can solve uniquely. If D = 0, the system is singular; then maybe infinite solutions or none. But problem says f(x) can be uniquely determined for all values except f(alpha) and f(beta) for some real numbers alpha and beta. So there are two values where determinant zero (i.e., x such that (x(1 - x))^2 = 1). For those x, the system may not determine f uniquely (maybe infinite solutions). So we need to find alpha and beta solving (x(1 - x))^2 = 1. Solve for x real.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708637074,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70863818,"data":"       | Let t = x(1 - x) = x - x^2. Then t^2 = 1 =\u003e t = ±1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708639466,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708640573,"data":"       | So need solutions of x - x^2 = 1 or -1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708641973,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.7086431,"data":"       | Equation x - x^2 = 1 =\u003e -x^2 + x - 1 = 0 =\u003e x^2 - x + 1 = 0. Discriminant Δ = (-1)^2 - 4 = -3 \u003c 0. No real solutions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708644524,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708645651,"data":"       | Equation x - x^2 = -1 =\u003e -x^2 + x + 1 = 0 =\u003e x^2 - x - 1 = 0 =\u003e solutions x = (1 ± sqrt(1 + 4))/2 = (1 ± sqrt5)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70864716,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708648232,"data":"       | Thus the two real numbers are alpha = (1 + sqrt5)/2 and beta = (1 - sqrt5)/2 (or vice versa). Indeed these are golden ratio φ and 1-φ = -1/φ? Wait compute: (1+√5)/2 ≈ 1.618; (1-√5)/2 ≈ -0.618.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708649869,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708650964,"data":"       | Thus α^2 + β^2 = ?\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708652111,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708653201,"data":"       | Compute: α^2 + β^2 = ((1+√5)/2)^2 + ((1-√5)/2)^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708654498,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708655578,"data":"       | Let’s compute symbolic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708656785,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708657852,"data":"       | We can compute manually: ( (1+√5)^2 + (1-√5)^2 ) / 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708659094,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708660172,"data":"       | Now (1+√5)^2 = 1 + 2√5 + 5 = 6 + 2√5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708661419,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708662479,"data":"       | (1-√5)^2 = 1 - 2√5 + 5 = 6 - 2√5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70866368,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708664766,"data":"       | Sum = (6+2√5)+(6-2√5)=12.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708665985,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708667033,"data":"       | Thus numerator 12, denominator 4 =\u003e 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708668231,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708669342,"data":"       | Thus α^2 + β^2 = 3.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708670482,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70867163,"data":"       | But double-check: Are there any other singularities? The determinant zero leads to not uniquely solvable; but maybe also the system may be inconsistent for those x? Let's verify that for those x, the two equations are consistent (i.e., same line). Need to check if RHS also satisfy same relation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708673248,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708674396,"data":"       | When D=0, we have x(1 - x) = ±1. For our real solutions, we have x - x^2 = -1 =\u003e x(1 - x) = -1. So product is -1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708677386,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708678802,"data":"       | Now we can see if equations (1) and (2) are dependent. Let's compute: Multiply equation (1) by (1 - x)^2 and equation (2) by 1, maybe? Actually matrix singular means rows are linearly dependent: there exists λ such that [x^2, 1] = λ [1, (1 - x)^2]. For D=0, we have x^2*(1 - x)^2 = 1. So λ = x^2 = 1/( (1 - x)^2 ). Indeed λ = x^2. Check: x^2 *? Wait: Row1 = [x^2, 1]; Row2 = [1, (1 - x)^2]. For them to be linearly dependent, there exists λ such that [x^2, 1] = λ [1, (1 - x)^2] =\u003e x^2 = λ, 1 = λ (1 - x)^2 =\u003e λ = 1/( (1 - x)^2 ). So x^2 = 1/( (1 - x)^2 ) =\u003e x^2 (1 - x)^2 = 1, which holds. So λ = x^2 = 1/( (1 - x)^2 ). Indeed consistent.\n"}
