,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.70886328,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4291.708864523,"data":"       | If D ≠ 0, we can solve uniquely. If D = 0, the system is singular; there may be infinite solutions or none. Since the functional equation is given, there must be at least one solution; but uniqueness fails (i.e., we cannot uniquely determine f(x) and f(1 - x)). So α, β are the x-values where D=0? Wait, D=0 yields singular matrix; but we have two unknowns f(x) and f(1 - x). However, note that for each x, we have two equations (the original and its transformed). But the functional equation only requires the original equation for all x; we derived the second equation by substituting x -\u003e 1 - x, which is also valid for all x (since the original holds for all real numbers). So indeed we have both equations for each x. So we have a system of two equations for each pair (x, 1 - x). However, note that the second equation is not independent if we treat x variable; it's just the same equation evaluated at 1 - x. So for each x, we have both equations, but they involve same pair of unknowns. So the system is as above.\n"}
