,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.191728727,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse the problem: A parallelogram has three of its vertices at (-1,0), (2,4), and (2,-4). We need the positive difference between the greatest possible perimeter and the least possible perimeter of the parallelogram.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.191730168,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.191731222,"data":"       | We have three known vertices of a parallelogram. The fourth vertex is unknown. There are multiple possible positions for the fourth vertex because the given three points could be arranged in different ways as vertices of a parallelogram. We need to consider all possible parallelograms with those three vertices and compute perimeters, then find max and min perimeters, then difference.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.191732901,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.191733982,"data":"       | We need to think: In a parallelogram, opposite sides are parallel and equal in length. Given three vertices A, B, C, there are generally three possible parallelograms where these three points are vertices: the unknown fourth vertex D can be placed such that the three given points correspond to any three of the four vertices. However, there are more possibilities: The given three points could be arranged such that one of them is the intersection of two sides (i.e., a vertex) but there are multiple ways to assign them as adjacent vertices or opposite vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.191735904,"data":"       | \n"}
