,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192742139,"data":"       | analysisWe got perimeters: case A (common vertex A) gives perimeter 20. Cases B and C give 26 each (same). So greatest perimeter = 26, least = 20, difference = 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192743456,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192744534,"data":"       | But need to double-check that we considered all possible parallelograms; maybe there could be more due to labeling of vertices differently? Actually given three vertices, there are exactly three possible parallelograms (since you can choose which point is the intersection of the two sides). However there might be also the possibility that the three points are collinear? Not here—they are not collinear.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.19274631,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192747314,"data":"       | Thus answer difference 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192748437,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.19274945,"data":"       | But we should double-check calculations manually.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192750566,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192751581,"data":"       | Compute distances:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192752646,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192753626,"data":"       | Given points: A(-1,0), B(2,4), C(2,-4).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192754741,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192755875,"data":"       | Case A: common vertex A. Then D = B + C - A = (2+2 - (-1), 4 + (-4) - 0) = (5,0). So D = (5,0). Quadrilateral order A-B-D-C (makes sense). Let's compute side lengths:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192757175,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192758201,"data":"       | AB distance: sqrt((2+1)^2 + (4-0)^2) = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = 5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192759369,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192760395,"data":"       | BD distance: B to D: (5-2, 0-4) = (3, -4) length sqrt(9+16)=5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192761575,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192762574,"data":"       | DC distance: D to C: (2-5, -4-0)=(-3,-4) length sqrt(9+16)=5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192763731,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192764741,"data":"       | CA distance: C to A: (-1-2, 0-(-4)) = (-3,4) length 5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192765987,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192768881,"data":"       | Thus all sides length 5? Wait we have a rhombus with side length 5, perimeter 20. Indeed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192770129,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192771169,"data":"       | Case B: common vertex B. D = A + C - B = (-1+2-2, 0+(-4)-4) = (-1, -8). Quadrilateral order B-A-D-C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192772414,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192773431,"data":"       | Compute side lengths:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.19277449,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192785922,"data":"       | BA: distance between B(2,4) and A(-1,0) = sqrt(3^2+4^2)=5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192787723,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192788766,"data":"       | AD: A(-1,0) to D(-1,-8) = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192789863,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192790877,"data":"       | DC: D(-1,-8) to C(2,-4) = sqrt(3^2+4^2)=5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192792001,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.19279304,"data":"       | CB: C(2,-4) to B(2,4) = 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192794371,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192795377,"data":"       | Thus sides lengths: 5,8,5,8 perimeter 26.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192796472,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192797502,"data":"       | Case C: common vertex C. D = A + B - C = (-1+2-2, 0+4-(-4)) = (-1,8). Quadrilateral order C-A-D-B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192798691,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192799734,"data":"       | Side lengths: CA = 5, AD = 8, DB = 5, BC = 8. Same perimeter 26.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192800864,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192802057,"data":"       | Thus difference 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192803113,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192804259,"data":"       | But need to ensure we considered \"positive difference between greatest possible perimeter and least possible perimeter\". So answer 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192805627,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192806699,"data":"       | Edge Cases: Could there be degenerate parallelogram where three points are not adjacent but maybe they could be opposite vertices? Actually if given three vertices, you could also have configuration where two of them are opposite vertices and third is adjacent to one of them? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192808219,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192809281,"data":"       | Given three points, there are three possibilities for the fourth vertex, as we derived. However there is also possibility that the three given points are not all adjacent; for example they could be two opposite vertices and one adjacent to one of them. But does that produce a different fourth vertex? Let's test.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192810892,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192811942,"data":"       | Suppose we have points A, B, C where A and B are opposite vertices (diagonal). Then the midpoint of AB must be same as midpoint of the other diagonal, which passes through C and unknown D. So D = 2*midpoint - C = (A+B) - C. Indeed that's same formula as case where C is common vertex? Wait if A and B are opposite, then D = A + B - C (since midpoint of AB is (A+B)/2, then D = 2*midpoint - C = A + B - C). That's exactly D3 (when C is common vertex). So that case is already accounted for.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192815817,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192816872,"data":"       | Thus three possibilities cover all.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192817975,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192819,"data":"       | Thus answer 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.19282005,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192821069,"data":"       | But we need to output as integer inside box.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192822207,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192823245,"data":"       | Let's produce final answer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192824335,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192825374,"data":"       | assistantfinalThe three given vertices are  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192826483,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192827526,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192828577,"data":"       | A=(-1,0),\\qquad B=(2,4),\\qquad C=(2,-4).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192829744,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.19283078,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192831821,"data":"       | For a parallelogram, the fourth vertex is determined by which of the three given points is the common vertex of the two adjacent sides.  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192833128,"data":"       | If \\(X\\) is the common vertex, then the opposite vertex is  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192834362,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.19283538,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192836457,"data":"       | D = Y+Z-X,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192837485,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192838532,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192840225,"data":"       | where \\(Y\\) and \\(Z\\) are the other two points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.19284151,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192842507,"data":"       | ---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192843524,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192844637,"data":"       | ### 1.  \\(A\\) is the common vertex  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192845744,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192846757,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192847816,"data":"       | D_1 = B+C-A = (2+2-(-1),\\;4+(-4)-0) = (5,0).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192848962,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.19285,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192851012,"data":"       | Ordering the vertices \\(A\\!-\\!B\\!-\\!D_1\\!-\\!C\\) gives side lengths  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192852309,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192853322,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192854368,"data":"       | AB = BC = CD = DA = \\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192855522,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192856541,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192857543,"data":"       | Perimeter  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192858564,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192859559,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192860602,"data":"       | P_1 = 4\\cdot5 = 20.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192863221,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192864288,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192865292,"data":"       | ---\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192866315,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192867333,"data":"       | ### 2.  \\(B\\) is the common vertex  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192868417,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192869408,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192870462,"data":"       | D_2 = A+C-B = (-1+2-2,\\;0+(-4)-4)=(-1,-8).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192871614,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192872631,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192873654,"data":"       | Vertices \\(B\\!-\\!A\\!-\\!D_2\\!-\\!C\\) give side lengths  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192874899,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192875915,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192876947,"data":"       | BA = 5,\\; AD_2 = 8,\\; D_2C = 5,\\; CB = 8,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192878159,"data":"       | \\]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192879167,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192880179,"data":"       | so  \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192881241,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192882236,"data":"       | \\[\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.192883252,"data":"       | P_2 = 5+8+5+8 = 26.\n"}
