,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193224818,"data":"       | analysisWe need to understand problem: Parallelogram with three vertices known: A = (-1,0), B = (2,4), C = (2,-4). Need to consider all possible parallelograms that have these three points as vertices (in any order). Determine the possible perimeters; find greatest possible perimeter and least possible perimeter; compute positive difference.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193226411,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193227462,"data":"       | We have three points; they could be any three vertices of a parallelogram. In a parallelogram, opposite sides are parallel and equal length; vertices order around shape.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193228784,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193230081,"data":"       | Given three points, there are two possible ways to complete parallelogram: The missing fourth vertex is determined uniquely depending on which of the three points is the common vertex of the two adjacent sides (i.e., which point is the \"shared\" vertex). Actually, given three points, there are three possible parallelograms: each point could be the intersection of the two known sides (i.e., the \"corner\" where the two known sides meet). For each choice, the missing vertex is determined by vector addition: If we have vertices P, Q, R known, and we assume they correspond to adjacent vertices P (common), Q adjacent to P, and R adjacent to P (i.e., P is the shared vertex). Then the fourth vertex S = Q + R - P (vector addition). That's a parallelogram with vertices P, Q, S, R (order). Similarly for each choice of shared vertex.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193234296,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193235357,"data":"       | Thus there are three possible parallelograms (maybe degenerate if points collinear). Let's compute perimeters for each.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193236615,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193238008,"data":"       | But also note that the three given points could be arranged such that two are opposite vertices and the third is adjacent to both? Wait in a parallelogram, any three vertices could be: either they include a pair of opposite vertices plus one adjacent to one of them (i.e., they could be not all adjacent). But given three points, there are two possibilities: either the missing vertex is determined by the vector addition as above (if the three points are not collinear). However, there is also possibility that the three points are not forming a \"V\" shape but could be two opposite vertices and a third vertex adjacent to one of them; but that still corresponds to one of the three choices for which point is the common vertex? Let's think: Suppose we have parallelogram ABCD with vertices in order. If we are given A, B, C (consecutive) then missing D = A + C - B (since B is common vertex). If we are given A, B, D (non-consecutive) (i.e., A and C opposite, B adjacent to A, D adjacent to A) Actually A, B, D includes A (common vertex) and two adjacent vertices B and D, missing C = B + D - A. That's same as case where A is common vertex. So any set of three vertices includes at least one vertex that is adjacent to the other two (i.e., the \"corner\"). Indeed, in any parallelogram, each vertex is adjacent to two others, opposite to one. So given any three vertices, there is at least one vertex that is adjacent to the other two (unless the three vertices are all collinear which can't happen). So we can treat each point as possible common vertex.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193241727,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193242744,"data":"       | Thus three possible parallelograms.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193243801,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193244849,"data":"       | Compute perimeters: For each configuration, we need side lengths: two distinct side lengths (adjacent sides). Since opposite sides equal, perimeter = 2*(length of side1 + length of side2). For each configuration, side1 = distance between common vertex and first adjacent vertex; side2 = distance between common vertex and second adjacent vertex. So perimeter = 2*(d(common, adj1) + d(common, adj2)). That's independent of the location of missing vertex.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193246626,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193247643,"data":"       | Thus for each choice of common vertex, compute sum of distances from that point to the other two points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193248824,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":4320.193249889,"data":"       | Given points: A = (-1,0), B = (2,4), C = (2,-4). Let's compute distances.\n"}
